ironments#Python\"\n      uid=1_530 button \"[show]\" expandable\n      uid=1_531 link \"Python implementations\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#Implementations\"\n      uid=1_533 button \"[show]\" expandable\n      uid=1_534 \"See also\"\n      uid=1_535 link \"icon\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Octicons-terminal.svg\"\n        uid=1_536 image \"icon\" url=\"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6f/Octicons-terminal.svg/20px-Octicons-terminal.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail\"\n      uid=1_537 \" \"\n      uid=1_538 link \"Computer programming portal\" description=\"Portal:Computer programming\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Portal:Computer_programming\"\n      uid=1_542 link \"Python Programming (Wikibook)\" description=\"b:Python Programming\" url=\"https://en.wikibooks.org/wiki/Python%20Programming\"\n      uid=1_544 link \"v\" description=\"Template:Python sidebar\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Template:Python_sidebar\"\n      uid=1_546 \" · \"\n      uid=1_547 link \"t\" description=\"Template talk:Python sidebar (page does not exist)\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Template_talk:Python_sidebar?action=edit&redlink=1\"\n      uid=1_546 \" · \"\n      uid=1_549 link \"e\" description=\"Special:EditPage/Template:Python sidebar\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Special:EditPage/Template:Python_sidebar\"\n    uid=1_551 \"Python\"\n    uid=1_552 \" is a \"\n    uid=1_553 link \"high-level\" description=\"High-level programming language\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/High-level_programming_language\"\n    uid=1_555 \", \"\n    uid=1_556 link \"general-purpose programming language\" description=\"General-purpose programming language\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/General-purpose_programming_language\"\n    uid=1_558 \" that emphasizes \"\n    uid=1_559 link \"code readability\" description=\"Code readability\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Code_readability\"\n    uid=1_561 \", simplicity, and ease-of-writing with the use of \"\n    uid=1_562 link \"significant indentation\" description=\"Significant indentation\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Significant_indentation\"\n    uid=1_564 \",\"\n    uid=1_565 link \"[38]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-AutoNT-7-40\"\n    uid=1_569 \" an extensive (\"batteries-included\") \"\n    uid=1_570 link \"standard library\" description=\"Standard library\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Standard_library\"\n    uid=1_572 \", and \"\n    uid=1_573 link \"garbage collection\" description=\"Garbage collection (computer science)\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Garbage_collection_(computer_science)\"\n    uid=1_575 \". Python supports multiple \"\n    uid=1_576 link \"programming paradigms\" description=\"Programming paradigm\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Programming_paradigm\"\n    uid=1_578 \" but with an emphasis on \"\n    uid=1_579 link \"object-oriented programming\" description=\"Object-oriented programming\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Object-oriented_programming\"\n    uid=1_581 \" and \"\n    uid=1_582 link \"dynamic typing\" description=\"Dynamic typing\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dynamic_typing\"\n    uid=1_584 \".\"\n    uid=1_585 link \"Guido van Rossum\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Guido_van_Rossum\"\n    uid=1_587 \" began working on Python in the late 1980s as a successor to the \"\n    uid=1_588 link \"ABC\" description=\"ABC (programming language)\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ABC_(programming_language)\"\n    uid=1_590 \" programming language. Python\"\n    uid=1_591 \" \"\n    uid=1_592 \"3.0, released in 2008, was a major revision and not completely \"\n    uid=1_593 link \"backward-compatible\" description=\"Backward-compatible\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Backward-compatible\"\n    uid=1_595 \" with earlier versions. Beginning with Python 3.5,\"\n    uid=1_596 link \"[39]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-41\"\n    uid=1_600 \" capabilities and keywords for typing were added to the language, allowing optional \"\n    uid=1_601 link \"static typing\" description=\"Static typing\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Static_typing\"\n    uid=1_603 \".\"\n    uid=1_604 link \"[40]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-42\"\n    uid=1_608 \" \"\n    uid=1_609 \"As of 2026\"\n    uid=1_610 \", the \"\n    uid=1_611 link \"Python Software Foundation\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_Software_Foundation\"\n    uid=1_613 \" supports Python 3.11, 3.12, 3.13, 3.14, and 3.15, following the project's annual release cycle and five-year support policy. Python 3.15.0 the latest stable version defaults to \"\n    uid=1_614 link \"UTF-8\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/UTF-8\"\n    uid=1_616 \", unlike previous versions. Earlier versions in the 3.x series have reached end-of-life and no longer receive security updates.\"\n    uid=1_617 \"Python is widely taught as an introductory programming language.\"\n    uid=1_618 link \"[41]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-43\"\n    uid=1_622 region \"History\"\n      uid=1_623 heading \"History\" level=\"2\"\n      uid=1_624 link \"edit\" description=\"Edit section: History\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Python_(programming_language)&action=edit&section=1\"\n      uid=1_626 \"Main article: \"\n      uid=1_627 link \"History of Python\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_Python\"\n      uid=1_629 link url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Guido_van_Rossum_in_PyConUS24.jpg\"\n      uid=1_630 \"The designer of Python, \"\n      uid=1_631 link \"Guido van Rossum\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Guido_van_Rossum\"\n      uid=1_633 \", at \"\n      uid=1_634 link \"PyCon\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/PyCon\"\n      uid=1_636 \" US 2024\"\n      uid=1_637 \"Python was conceived in the late 1980s\"\n      uid=1_638 link \"[11]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-venners-interview-pt-1-13\"\n      uid=1_642 \" by \"\n      uid=1_643 link \"Guido van Rossum\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Guido_van_Rossum\"\n      uid=1_645 \" at \"\n      uid=1_646 link \"Centrum Wiskunde & Informatica\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Centrum_Wiskunde_&_Informatica\"\n      uid=1_648 \" (CWI) in the \"\n      uid=1_649 link \"Netherlands\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Netherlands\"\n      uid=1_651 \".\"\n      uid=1_652 link \"[42]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-timeline-of-python-44\"\n      uid=1_656 \" It was designed as a successor to the \"\n      uid=1_657 link \"ABC\" description=\"ABC (programming language)\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ABC_(programming_language)\"\n      uid=1_659 \" programming language, which was inspired by \"\n      uid=1_660 link \"SETL\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SETL\"\n      uid=1_662 \",\"\n      uid=1_663 link \"[43]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-AutoNT-12-45\"\n      uid=1_667 \" capable of \"\n      uid=1_668 link \"exception handling\" description=\"Exception handling\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Exception_handling\"\n      uid=1_670 \" and interfacing with the \"\n      uid=1_671 link \"Amoeba\" description=\"Amoeba (operating system)\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amoeba_(operating_system)\"\n      uid=1_673 \" operating system.\"\n      uid=1_674 link \"[18]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-faq-created-20\"\n      uid=1_678 \" Python implementation began in December\"\n      uid=1_679 \" \"\n      uid=1_680 \"1989.\"\n      uid=1_681 link \"[42]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-timeline-of-python-44\"\n      uid=1_685 \" Van Rossum first released it in 1991 as Python\"\n      uid=1_686 \" \"\n      uid=1_687 \"0.9.0.\"\n      uid=1_688 link \"[42]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-timeline-of-python-44\"\n      uid=1_692 \" Van Rossum assumed sole responsibility for the project, as the lead developer, until 12 July 2018, when he announced his \"permanent vacation\" from responsibilities as Python's \"\"\n      uid=1_693 link \"benevolent dictator for life\" description=\"Benevolent dictator for life\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Benevolent_dictator_for_life\"\n      uid=1_695 \"\" (BDFL); this title was bestowed on him by the Python community to reflect his long-term commitment as the project's chief decision-maker.\"\n      uid=1_696 link \"[44]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-lj-bdfl-resignation-46\"\n      uid=1_700 link \"[c]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-47\"\n      uid=1_704 \" In January\"\n      uid=1_705 \" \"\n      uid=1_706 \"2019, active Python core developers elected a five-member Steering Council to lead the project.\"\n      uid=1_707 link \"[45]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-48\"\n      uid=1_711 link \"[46]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-49\"\n      uid=1_715 \"The name \"\n      uid=1_716 \"Python\"\n      uid=1_717 \" derives from the British comedy series \"\n      uid=1_718 link \"Monty Python's Flying Circus\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Monty_Python's_Flying_Circus\"\n      uid=1_720 \".\"\n      uid=1_721 link \"[47]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-:0-50\"\n      uid=1_725 \" (See \"\n      uid=1_726 link \"§ Naming\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#Naming\"\n      uid=1_730 \".)\"\n      uid=1_731 \"Python 2.0 was released on 16 October 2000, featuring many new features such as \"\n      uid=1_732 link \"list comprehensions\" description=\"List comprehension\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_comprehension\"\n      uid=1_734 \", \"\n      uid=1_735 link \"cycle-detecting\" description=\"Cycle detection\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cycle_detection\"\n      uid=1_737 \" garbage collection, \"\n      uid=1_738 link \"reference counting\" description=\"Reference counting\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Reference_counting\"\n      uid=1_740 \", and \"\n      uid=1_741 link \"Unicode\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Unicode\"\n      uid=1_743 \" support.\"\n      uid=1_744 link \"[48]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-newin-2.0-51\"\n      uid=1_748 \" Python 2.7's \"\n      uid=1_749 link \"end-of-life\" description=\"End-of-life product\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/End-of-life_product\"\n      uid=1_751 \" was initially set for 2015, and then postponed to 2020 out of concern that a large body of existing code could not easily be forward-ported to Python\"\n      uid=1_752 \" \"\n      uid=1_753 \"3.\"\n      uid=1_754 link \"[49]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-52\"\n      uid=1_758 link \"[50]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-53\"\n      uid=1_762 \" It no longer receives security patches or updates.\"\n      uid=1_763 link \"[51]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-54\"\n      uid=1_767 link \"[52]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-55\"\n      uid=1_771 \" While Python 2.7 and older versions are officially unsupported, a different unofficial Python implementation, \"\n      uid=1_772 link \"PyPy\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/PyPy\"\n      uid=1_774 \", continues to support Python 2, i.e., \"2.7.18+\" (plus 3.11 and 3.12\"\n      uid=1_775 \"), with the plus signifying (at least some) \"\"\n      uid=1_776 link \"backported\" description=\"Backported\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Backported\"\n      uid=1_778 \" security updates\".\"\n      uid=1_779 link \"[53]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-56\"\n      uid=1_783 \"Python\"\n      uid=1_784 \" \"\n      uid=1_785 \"3.0 was released on 3 December 2008, and was a major revision and not completely \"\n      uid=1_786 link \"backward-compatible\" description=\"Backward-compatible\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Backward-compatible\"\n      uid=1_788 \" with earlier versions, with some new semantics and changed syntax. Python\"\n      uid=1_789 \" \"\n      uid=1_790 \"2.7.18, released in 2020, was the last release of Python\"\n      uid=1_791 \" \"\n      uid=1_792 \"2.\"\n      uid=1_793 link \"[54]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-57\"\n      uid=1_797 \" Several releases in the Python 3.x series have added new syntax to the language, and made a few (considered very minor) backward-incompatible changes.\"\n      uid=1_798 \"As of October 2026\"\n      uid=1_799 \", Python 3.15.0 \"\n      uid=1_800 \"is the latest stable release, and before in same month 3.14.8 an \"expedited security release\"\"\n      uid=1_801 link \"[55]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-58\"\n      uid=1_805 \" is the latest stable release, and since 3.14 official Android binary releases are available. All older 3.x versions had a security update down to \"\n      uid=1_806 \"Python 3.10.22. Python 3.11 is, since October 2026, the oldest supported branch.\"\n      uid=1_807 link \"[56]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-59\"\n      uid=1_811 \" Python 3.15 adds e.g. \"\n      uid=1_812 \"frozendict\"\n      uid=1_813 \" and \"\n      uid=1_814 \"sentinel\"\n      uid=1_815 \" built-in types, and a new soft keyword, \"\n      uid=1_816 \"lazy\"\n      uid=1_817 \", for lazy imports.\"\n      uid=1_818 link \"[57]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-60\"\n      uid=1_822 \" Releases receive two years of full support followed by three years of security support.\"\n    uid=1_823 region \"Design philosophy and features\"\n      uid=1_824 heading \"Design philosophy and features\" level=\"2\"\n      uid=1_825 link \"edit\" description=\"Edit section: Design philosophy and features\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Python_(programming_language)&action=edit&section=2\"\n      uid=1_827 \"Python is a \"\n      uid=1_828 link \"multi-paradigm programming language\" description=\"Multi-paradigm programming language\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multi-paradigm_programming_language\"\n      uid=1_830 \". Object-oriented programming and structured programming are fully supported, and many of their features support functional programming and \"\n      uid=1_831 link \"aspect-oriented programming\" description=\"Aspect-oriented programming\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aspect-oriented_programming\"\n      uid=1_833 \" – including \"\n      uid=1_834 link \"metaprogramming\" description=\"Metaprogramming\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Metaprogramming\"\n      uid=1_836 link \"[58]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-AutoNT-13-61\"\n      uid=1_840 \" and \"\n      uid=1_841 link \"metaobjects\" description=\"Metaobject\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Metaobject\"\n      uid=1_843 \".\"\n      uid=1_844 link \"[59]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-AutoNT-14-62\"\n      uid=1_848 \" Many other paradigms are supported via extensions, including \"\n      uid=1_849 link \"design by contract\" description=\"Design by contract\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Design_by_contract\"\n      uid=1_851 link \"[60]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-AutoNT-15-63\"\n      uid=1_855 link \"[61]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-AutoNT-16-64\"\n      uid=1_859 \" and \"\n      uid=1_860 link \"logic programming\" description=\"Logic programming\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Logic_programming\"\n      uid=1_862 \".\"\n      uid=1_863 link \"[62]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-AutoNT-17-65\"\n      uid=1_867 \" Python is often referred to as a \"\n      uid=1_868 \"‘\"\n      uid=1_869 link \"glue language\" description=\"Glue language\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Glue_language\"\n      uid=1_871 \"’\"\n      uid=1_872 link \"[63]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-66\"\n      uid=1_876 \" because it is purposely designed to be able to integrate components written in other languages.\"\n      uid=1_877 \"Python uses dynamic typing and a combination of \"\n      uid=1_878 link \"reference counting\" description=\"Reference counting\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Reference_counting\"\n      uid=1_880 \" and a cycle-detecting garbage collector for \"\n      uid=1_881 link \"memory management\" description=\"Memory management\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Memory_management\"\n      uid=1_883 \".\"\n      uid=1_884 link \"[64]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-Reference_counting-67\"\n      uid=1_888 \" It uses dynamic \"\n      uid=1_889 link \"name resolution\" description=\"Name resolution (programming languages)\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Name_resolution_(programming_languages)\"\n      uid=1_891 \" (\"\n      uid=1_892 link \"late binding\" description=\"Late binding\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Late_binding\"\n      uid=1_894 \"), which binds method and variable names during program execution.\"\n      uid=1_895 \"Python's design offers some support for functional programming in the \"\"\n      uid=1_896 link \"Lisp\" description=\"Lisp (programming language)\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lisp_(programming_language)\"\n      uid=1_898 \" tradition\". It has \"\n      uid=1_899 \"filter\"\n      uid=1_900 \", \"\n      uid=1_901 \"map\"\n      uid=1_902 \", and \"\n      uid=1_903 \"reduce\"\n      uid=1_904 \" functions; \"\n      uid=1_905 link \"list comprehensions\" description=\"List comprehension\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_comprehension\"\n      uid=1_907 \", \"\n      uid=1_908 link \"dictionaries\" description=\"Associative array\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Associative_array\"\n      uid=1_910 \", \"\n      uid=1_911 link \"sets\" description=\"Set (mathematics)\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Set_(mathematics)\"\n      uid=1_913 \", and \"\n      uid=1_914 link \"generator\" description=\"Generator (computer programming)\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Generator_(computer_programming)\"\n      uid=1_916 \" expressions.\"\n      uid=1_917 link \"[65]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-AutoNT-59-68\"\n      uid=1_921 \" The standard library has two modules (\"\n      uid=1_922 \"itertools\"\n      uid=1_923 \" and \"\n      uid=1_924 \"functools\"\n      uid=1_925 \") that implement functional tools borrowed from \"\n      uid=1_926 link \"Haskell\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Haskell\"\n      uid=1_928 \" and \"\n      uid=1_929 link \"Standard ML\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Standard_ML\"\n      uid=1_931 \".\"\n      uid=1_932 link \"[66]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-AutoNT-18-69\"\n      uid=1_936 \"Python's core philosophy is summarized in the \"\n      uid=1_937 link \"Zen of Python\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zen_of_Python\"\n      uid=1_939 \" (PEP 20) written by \"\n      uid=1_940 link \"Tim Peters\" description=\"Tim Peters (software engineer)\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tim_Peters_(software_engineer)\"\n      uid=1_942 \", which includes aphorisms such as these:\"\n      uid=1_943 link \"[67]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-PEP20-70\"\n      uid=1_947 \"Explicit is better than implicit.\"\n      uid=1_948 \"Simple is better than complex.\"\n      uid=1_949 \"Readability counts.\"\n      uid=1_950 \"Special cases aren't special enough to break the rules.\"\n      uid=1_951 \"Although practicality beats purity, errors should never pass silently, unless explicitly silenced.\"\n      uid=1_952 \"There should be one—and preferably only one—obvious way to do it.\"\n      uid=1_953 \"However, Python has received criticism for violating these principles and adding unnecessary language bloat.\"\n      uid=1_954 link \"[68]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-Python-Changes-2014-71\"\n      uid=1_958 \" Responses to these criticisms note that the Zen of Python is a guideline rather than a rule.\"\n      uid=1_959 link \"[69]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-Confusion-regarding-a-rule-in-the-Zen-of-Python-72\"\n      uid=1_963 \" The addition of some new features had been controversial: Guido van Rossum resigned as \"\n      uid=1_964 \"Benevolent Dictator for Life\"\n      uid=1_965 \" after conflict about adding the assignment expression operator in \"\n      uid=1_966 \"Python 3.8.\"\n      uid=1_967 link \"[70]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-The-Most-Controversial-Python-Walrus-Operator-73\"\n      uid=1_971 link \"[71]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-The-Controversy-Behind-The-Walrus-Operator-in-Python-74\"\n      uid=1_975 \"Nevertheless, rather than building all functionality into its core, Python was designed to be highly \"\n      uid=1_976 link \"extensible\" description=\"Extensible\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Extensible\"\n      uid=1_978 \" through modules. This compact modularity has made it particularly popular as a means of adding programmable interfaces to existing applications. Van Rossum's vision of a small core language with a large standard library and an easily extensible interpreter stemmed from his frustrations with ABC, which represented the opposite approach.\"\n      uid=1_979 link \"[11]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-venners-interview-pt-1-13\"\n      uid=1_983 \"Python claims to strive for a simpler, less-cluttered \"\n      uid=1_984 link \"syntax\" description=\"Syntax (programming languages)\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Syntax_(programming_languages)\"\n      uid=1_986 \" and grammar, while giving developers a choice in their coding methodology. Python lacks \"\n      uid=1_987 link \"do .. while loops\" description=\"Loop (statement)\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Loop_(statement)#Post-test_loop\"\n      uid=1_990 \", which \"\n      uid=1_991 link \"Rossum\" description=\"Guido Van Rossum\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Guido_Van_Rossum\"\n      uid=1_993 \" considered harmful.\"\n      uid=1_994 link \"[72]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-75\"\n      uid=1_998 \" In contrast to \"\n      uid=1_999 link \"Perl\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Perl\"\n      uid=1_1001 \"'s motto \"\"\n      uid=1_1002 link \"there is more than one way to do it\" description=\"There is more than one way to do it\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/There_is_more_than_one_way_to_do_it\"\n      uid=1_1004 \"\", Python advocates an approach where \"there should be one – and preferably only one – obvious way to do it\".\"\n      uid=1_1005 link \"[67]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-PEP20-70\"\n      uid=1_1009 \" In practice, however, Python provides many ways to achieve a given goal. There are at least three ways to format a string literal, with no certainty as to which one a programmer should use.\"\n      uid=1_1010 link \"[73]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-Python-String-Formatting-Best-Practices-76\"\n      uid=1_1014 \" \"\n      uid=1_1015 link \"Alex Martelli\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alex_Martelli\"\n      uid=1_1017 \" is a \"\n      uid=1_1018 link \"Fellow\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fellow\"\n      uid=1_1020 \" at the \"\n      uid=1_1021 link \"Python Software Foundation\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_Software_Foundation\"\n      uid=1_1023 \" and Python book author; he wrote that \"To describe something as 'clever' is \"\n      uid=1_1024 \"not\"\n      uid=1_1025 \" considered a compliment in the Python culture.\"\"\n      uid=1_1026 link \"[74]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-AutoNT-19-77\"\n      uid=1_1030 \"Python's developers typically prioritize readability over performance. For example, they reject patches to non-critical parts of the \"\n      uid=1_1031 link \"CPython\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/CPython\"\n      uid=1_1033 \" reference implementation that would offer increases in speed that do not justify the cost of clarity and readability.\"\n      uid=1_1034 link \"[75]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-AutoNT-20-78\"\n      uid=1_1038 \"[\"\n      uid=1_1039 link \"failed verification\" description=\"Wikipedia:Verifiability\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Verifiability\"\n      uid=1_1041 \"]\"\n      uid=1_1042 \" Execution speed can be improved by moving speed-critical functions to extension modules written in languages such as \"\n      uid=1_1043 link \"C\" description=\"C (programming language)\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/C_(programming_language)\"\n      uid=1_1045 \", or by using a \"\n      uid=1_1046 link \"just-in-time compiler\" description=\"Just-in-time compiler\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Just-in-time_compiler\"\n      uid=1_1048 \" like \"\n      uid=1_1049 link \"PyPy\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/PyPy\"\n      uid=1_1051 \". Also, it is possible to transpile to other languages. However, this approach either fails to achieve the expected speed-up, since Python is a very \"\n      uid=1_1052 link \"dynamic language\" description=\"Dynamic language\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dynamic_language\"\n      uid=1_1054 \", or only a restricted subset of Python is compiled (with potential minor semantic changes).\"\n      uid=1_1055 link \"[76]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-PyJL-79\"\n      uid=1_1059 \"Python is meant to be a fun language to use.\"\n      uid=1_1060 link \"[77]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-fun-80\"\n      uid=1_1064 \":\"\n      uid=1_1065 \" \"\n      uid=1_1066 \"3\"\n      uid=1_1067 \" \"\n      uid=1_1068 \" This goal is reflected in the name – a tribute to the British comedy group \"\n      uid=1_1069 link \"Monty Python\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Monty_Python\"\n      uid=1_1071 link \"[78]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-whyname-81\"\n      uid=1_1075 \" – and in playful approaches to some tutorials and reference materials. For instance, some code examples use the terms \"spam\" and \"eggs\" (in reference to \"\n      uid=1_1076 link \"a Monty Python sketch\" description=\"Spam (Monty Python)\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spam_(Monty_Python)\"\n      uid=1_1078 \"), rather than the typical terms \"\n      uid=1_1079 link \"\"foo\" and \"bar\"\" description=\"Foobar\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foobar\"\n      uid=1_1081 \".\"\n      uid=1_1082 link \"[77]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-fun-80\"\n      uid=1_1086 link \"[79]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-pprint-doc-82\"\n      uid=1_1090 \"A common \"\n      uid=1_1091 link \"neologism\" description=\"Neologism\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Neologism\"\n      uid=1_1093 \" in the Python community is \"\n      uid=1_1094 \"pythonic\"\n      uid=1_1095 \", which has a broad range of meanings related to program style: Pythonic code may use Python \"\n      uid=1_1096 link \"idioms\" description=\"Programming idiom\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Programming_idiom\"\n      uid=1_1098 \" well; be natural or show fluency in the language; or conform with Python's minimalist philosophy and emphasis on readability.\"\n      uid=1_1099 link \"[80]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-83\"\n      uid=1_1103 region \"Enhancement Proposals\"\n        uid=1_1104 heading \"Enhancement Proposals\" level=\"3\"\n        uid=1_1105 link \"edit\" description=\"Edit section: Enhancement Proposals\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Python_(programming_language)&action=edit&section=3\"\n        uid=1_1107 \"Python Enhancement Proposals\"\n        uid=1_1108 link \"[note 1]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-85\"\n        uid=1_1112 \" are a design document for either providing information to the \"\n        uid=1_1113 link \"Python\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)\"\n        uid=1_1115 \" community, or proposal for new feature in Python.\"\n        uid=1_1116 link \"[81]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-PEP1-84\"\n        uid=1_1120 \" PEPs are intended to explain new processes in Python, provide \"\n        uid=1_1121 link \"naming conventions\" description=\"Naming convention (programming)\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Naming_convention_(programming)\"\n        uid=1_1123 \" or document the processes in the language.\"\n        uid=1_1124 link \"[82]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-PyGlossary-86\"\n        uid=1_1128 \" PEPs are overseen by Python Steering Council.\"\n        uid=1_1129 link \"[82]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-PyGlossary-86\"\n        uid=1_1133 \"There are 3 kinds of PEPs, with those are being \"\n        uid=1_1134 \"standards track PEP\"\n        uid=1_1135 link \"[note 2]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-87\"\n        uid=1_1139 \", \"\n        uid=1_1140 \"Informational PEP\"\n        uid=1_1141 link \"[note 3]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-88\"\n        uid=1_1145 \" and \"\n        uid=1_1146 \"Process PEP\"\n        uid=1_1147 \"s\"\n        uid=1_1148 link \"[note 4]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-89\"\n        uid=1_1152 \" which has their own unique meanings.\"\n        uid=1_1153 link \"[81]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-PEP1-84\"\n        uid=1_1157 link \"[83]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-believemy-90\"\n        uid=1_1161 \" They were firstly introduced in 2000, inspired by other RfCs (requests for comments) and Design Enhancement Proposals.\"\n        uid=1_1162 link \"[83]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-believemy-90\"\n        uid=1_1166 \" Most known PEPs are PEP\"\n        uid=1_1167 \" \"\n        uid=1_1168 \"–\"\n        uid=1_1169 \" \"\n        uid=1_1170 \"1, PEP\"\n        uid=1_1171 \" \"\n        uid=1_1172 \"–\"\n        uid=1_1173 \" \"\n        uid=1_1174 \"8, \"\n        uid=1_1175 link \"PEP – 20\" description=\"Zen of Python\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zen_of_Python\"\n        uid=1_1181 \", PEP\"\n        uid=1_1182 \" \"\n        uid=1_1183 \"–\"\n        uid=1_1184 \" \"\n        uid=1_1185 \"257 and others.\"\n        uid=1_1186 link \"[83]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-believemy-90\"\n    uid=1_1190 region \"Syntax and semantics\"\n      uid=1_1191 heading \"Syntax and semantics\" level=\"2\"\n      uid=1_1192 link \"edit\" description=\"Edit section: Syntax and semantics\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Python_(programming_language)&action=edit&section=4\"\n      uid=1_1194 \"Main article: \"\n      uid=1_1195 link \"Python syntax and semantics\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_syntax_and_semantics\"\n      uid=1_1197 \"Python is meant to be an easily readable language. Its formatting is visually uncluttered and often uses English keywords where other languages use punctuation. Unlike many other languages, it does not use \"\n      uid=1_1198 link \"curly brackets\" description=\"Curly bracket programming language\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Curly_bracket_programming_language\"\n      uid=1_1200 \" to delimit blocks, and semicolons after statements are allowed but rarely used. It has fewer syntactic exceptions and special cases than \"\n      uid=1_1201 link \"C\" description=\"C (programming language)\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/C_(programming_language)\"\n      uid=1_1203 \" or \"\n      uid=1_1204 link \"Pascal\" description=\"Pascal (programming language)\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pascal_(programming_language)\"\n      uid=1_1206 \".\"\n      uid=1_1207 link \"[84]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-AutoNT-52-91\"\n      uid=1_1211 region \"Indentation\"\n        uid=1_1212 heading \"Indentation\" level=\"3\"\n        uid=1_1213 link \"edit\" description=\"Edit section: Indentation\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Python_(programming_language)&action=edit&section=5\"\n        uid=1_1215 \"Further information: \"\n        uid=1_1216 link \"Python syntax and semantics § Indentation\" description=\"Python syntax and semantics\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_syntax_and_semantics#Indentation\"\n        uid=1_1220 \"Python uses \"\n        uid=1_1221 link \"whitespace\" description=\"Whitespace character\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Whitespace_character\"\n        uid=1_1223 \" indentation, rather than curly brackets or keywords, to delimit \"\n        uid=1_1224 link \"blocks\" description=\"Block (programming)\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Block_(programming)\"\n        uid=1_1226 \". An increase in indentation comes after certain statements; a decrease in indentation signifies the end of the current block.\"\n        uid=1_1227 link \"[85]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-AutoNT-53-92\"\n        uid=1_1231 \" Thus, the program's visual structure accurately represents its semantic structure.\"\n        uid=1_1232 link \"[86]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-guttag-93\"\n        uid=1_1236 \" This feature is sometimes termed the \"\n        uid=1_1237 link \"off-side rule\" description=\"Off-side rule\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Off-side_rule\"\n        uid=1_1239 \". Some other languages use indentation this way; but in most, indentation has no semantic meaning. The recommended indent size is four spaces.\"\n        uid=1_1240 link \"[87]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-pep8-94\"\n      uid=1_1244 region \"Statements and control flow\"\n        uid=1_1245 heading \"Statements and control flow\" level=\"3\"\n        uid=1_1246 link \"edit\" description=\"Edit section: Statements and control flow\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Python_(programming_language)&action=edit&section=6\"\n        uid=1_1248 \"Python's \"\n        uid=1_1249 link \"statements\" description=\"Statement (computer science)\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Statement_(computer_science)\"\n        uid=1_1251 \" include the following:\"\n        uid=1_1252 \"The \"\n        uid=1_1253 link \"assignment\" description=\"Assignment (computer science)\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Assignment_(computer_science)\"\n        uid=1_1255 \" statement, using a single equals sign \"\n        uid=1_1256 \"=\"\n        uid=1_1257 \"The \"\n        uid=1_1258 link \"if\" description=\"If-then-else\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/If-then-else\"\n        uid=1_1260 \" statement, which conditionally executes a block of code, along with \"\n        uid=1_1261 link \"else\" description=\"Conditional (computer programming)\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Conditional_(computer_programming)#If%E2%80%93then(%E2%80%93else)\"\n        uid=1_1263 \" and \"\n        uid=1_1264 \"elif\"\n        uid=1_1265 \" (a contraction of \"\n        uid=1_1266 link \"else if\" description=\"Conditional (computer programming)\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Conditional_(computer_programming)#Else_if\"\n        uid=1_1268 \")\"\n        uid=1_1269 \"The \"\n        uid=1_1270 link \"for\" description=\"Foreach\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foreach#Python\"\n        uid=1_1272 \" statement, which iterates over an \"\n        uid=1_1273 \"iterable\"\n        uid=1_1274 \" object, capturing each element to a variable for use by the attached block; the variable is not deleted when the loop finishes\"\n        uid=1_1275 \"The \"\n        uid=1_1276 link \"while\" description=\"While loop\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/While_loop#Python\"\n        uid=1_1278 \" statement, which executes a block of code as long as boolean condition is true\"\n        uid=1_1279 \"The \"\n        uid=1_1280 link \"try\" description=\"Exception handling syntax\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Exception_handling_syntax#Python\"\n        uid=1_1282 \" statement, which allows exceptions raised in its attached code block to be caught and handled by \"\n        uid=1_1283 \"except\"\n        uid=1_1284 \" clauses (or new syntax \"\n        uid=1_1285 \"except*\"\n        uid=1_1286 \" in Python 3.11 for exception groups);\"\n        uid=1_1287 link \"[88]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-95\"\n        uid=1_1291 \" the \"\n        uid=1_1292 \"try\"\n        uid=1_1293 \" statement also ensures that clean-up code in a \"\n        uid=1_1294 \"finally\"\n        uid=1_1295 \" block is always run regardless of how the block exits\"\n        uid=1_1296 \"The \"\n        uid=1_1297 \"raise\"\n        uid=1_1298 \" statement, used to raise a specified exception or re-raise a caught exception\"\n        uid=1_1299 \"The \"\n        uid=1_1300 \"class\"\n        uid=1_1301 \" statement, which executes a block of code and attaches its local namespace to a \"\n        uid=1_1302 link \"class\" description=\"Class (computer science)\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Class_(computer_science)\"\n        uid=1_1304 \", for use in object-oriented programming\"\n        uid=1_1305 \"The \"\n        uid=1_1306 \"def\"\n        uid=1_1307 \" statement, which defines a \"\n        uid=1_1308 link \"function\" description=\"Function (computer programming)\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Function_(computer_programming)\"\n        uid=1_1310 \" or \"\n        uid=1_1311 link \"method\" description=\"Method (computing)\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Method_(computing)\"\n        uid=1_1313 \"The \"\n        uid=1_1314 link \"with\" description=\"Dispose pattern\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dispose_pattern#Language_constructs\"\n        uid=1_1316 \" statement, which encloses a code block within a context manager, allowing \"\n        uid=1_1317 link \"resource-acquisition-is-initialization\" description=\"Resource acquisition is initialization\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Resource_acquisition_is_initialization\"\n        uid=1_1319 \" (RAII)-like behavior and replacing a common try/finally idiom\"\n        uid=1_1320 link \"[89]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-96\"\n        uid=1_1324 \" Examples of a context include acquiring a \"\n        uid=1_1325 link \"lock\" description=\"Lock (computer science)\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lock_(computer_science)\"\n        uid=1_1327 \" before some code is run, and then releasing the lock; or opening and then closing a \"\n        uid=1_1328 link \"file\" description=\"Computer file\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Computer_file\"\n        uid=1_1330 \"The \"\n        uid=1_1331 link \"break\" description=\"Break statement\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Break_statement\"\n        uid=1_1333 \" statement, which exits a loop\"\n        uid=1_1334 \"The \"\n        uid=1_1335 \"continue\"\n        uid=1_1336 \" statement, which skips the rest of the current iteration and continues with the next\"\n        uid=1_1337 \"The \"\n        uid=1_1338 \"del\"\n        uid=1_1339 \" statement, which removes a variable—deleting the reference from the name to the value, and producing an error if the variable is referred to before it is redefined\"\n        uid=1_1340 link \"[d]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-97\"\n        uid=1_1344 \"The \"\n        uid=1_1345 \"pass\"\n        uid=1_1346 \" statement, serving as a \"\n        uid=1_1347 link \"NOP\" description=\"NOP (code)\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/NOP_(code)\"\n        uid=1_1349 \" (i.e., no operation), which is syntactically needed to create an empty code block\"\n        uid=1_1350 \"The \"\n        uid=1_1351 link \"assert\" description=\"Assertion (software development)\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Assertion_(software_development)\"\n        uid=1_1353 \" statement, used in debugging to check for conditions that should apply\"\n        uid=1_1354 \"The \"\n        uid=1_1355 \"yield\"\n        uid=1_1356 \" statement, which returns a value from a \"\n        uid=1_1357 link \"generator\" description=\"Generator (computer programming)\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Generator_(computer_programming)#Python\"\n        uid=1_1359 \" function (and also an operator); used to implement \"\n        uid=1_1360 link \"coroutines\" description=\"Coroutine\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coroutine\"\n        uid=1_1362 \"The \"\n        uid=1_1363 \"return\"\n        uid=1_1364 \" statement, used to return a value from a function\"\n        uid=1_1365 \"The \"\n        uid=1_1366 link \"import\" description=\"Include directive\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Include_directive\"\n        uid=1_1368 \" and \"\n        uid=1_1369 \"from\"\n        uid=1_1370 \" statements, used to import modules whose functions or variables can be used in the current program. Python 3.15 adds a new functionality to lazily import with a new keyword: \"The \"\n        uid=1_1371 \"lazy\"\n        uid=1_1372 \" keyword works with both \"\n        uid=1_1373 \"import\"\n        uid=1_1374 \" and \"\n        uid=1_1375 \"from ... import\"\n        uid=1_1376 \" statements.\"\"\n        uid=1_1377 link \"[90]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-Python3.15-whatsnew-98\"\n        uid=1_1381 \"The \"\n        uid=1_1382 \"match\"\n        uid=1_1383 \" and \"\n        uid=1_1384 \"case\"\n        uid=1_1385 \" statements, analogous to a \"\n        uid=1_1386 link \"switch statement\" description=\"Switch statement\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Switch_statement\"\n        uid=1_1388 \" construct, which compares an expression against one or more cases as a control-flow measure\"\n        uid=1_1389 \"The assignment statement (\"\n        uid=1_1390 \"=\"\n        uid=1_1391 \") binds a name as a \"\n        uid=1_1392 link \"reference\" description=\"Pointer (computer programming)\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pointer_(computer_programming)\"\n        uid=1_1394 \" to a separate, dynamically allocated \"\n        uid=1_1395 link \"object\" description=\"Object (computer science)\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Object_(computer_science)\"\n        uid=1_1397 \". Variables may subsequently be rebound at any time to any object. In Python, a variable name is a generic reference holder without a fixed \"\n        uid=1_1398 link \"data type\" description=\"Type system\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Type_system\"\n        uid=1_1400 \"; however, it always refers to \"\n        uid=1_1401 \"some\"\n        uid=1_1402 \" object with a type. This is called \"\n        uid=1_1403 link \"dynamic typing\" description=\"Type system\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Type_system#Dynamic_type_checking_and_runtime_type_information\"\n        uid=1_1405 \"—in contrast to \"\n        uid=1_1406 link \"statically-typed\" description=\"Statically-typed\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Statically-typed\"\n        uid=1_1408 \" languages, where each variable may contain only a value of a certain type.\"\n        uid=1_1409 \"Python does not support \"\n        uid=1_1410 link \"tail call\" description=\"Tail call\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tail_call\"\n        uid=1_1412 \" optimization or \"\n        uid=1_1413 link \"first-class continuations\" description=\"First-class continuations\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/First-class_continuations\"\n        uid=1_1415 \"; according to Van Rossum, the language never will.\"\n        uid=1_1416 link \"[91]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-AutoNT-55-99\"\n        uid=1_1420 link \"[92]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-AutoNT-56-100\"\n        uid=1_1424 \" However, better support for \"\n        uid=1_1425 link \"coroutine\" description=\"Coroutine\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coroutine\"\n        uid=1_1427 \"-like functionality is provided by extending Python's generators.\"\n        uid=1_1428 link \"[93]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-AutoNT-57-101\"\n        uid=1_1432 \" Before 2.5, generators were \"\n        uid=1_1433 link \"lazy\" description=\"Lazy evaluation\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lazy_evaluation\"\n        uid=1_1435 \" \"\n        uid=1_1436 link \"iterators\" description=\"Iterator\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iterator\"\n        uid=1_1438 \"; data was passed unidirectionally out of the generator. From Python\"\n        uid=1_1439 \" \"\n        uid=1_1440 \"2.5 on, it is possible to pass data back into a generator function; and from version 3.3, data can be passed through multiple stack levels.\"\n        uid=1_1441 link \"[94]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-AutoNT-58-102\"\n      uid=1_1445 region \"Expressions\"\n        uid=1_1446 heading \"Expressions\" level=\"3\"\n        uid=1_1447 link \"edit\" description=\"Edit section: Expressions\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Python_(programming_language)&action=edit&section=7\"\n        uid=1_1449 \"Python's \"\n        uid=1_1450 link \"expressions\" description=\"Expression (computer science)\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Expression_(computer_science)\"\n        uid=1_1452 \" include the following:\"\n        uid=1_1453 \"The \"\n        uid=1_1454 \"+\"\n        uid=1_1455 \", \"\n        uid=1_1456 \"-\"\n        uid=1_1457 \", and \"\n        uid=1_1458 \"*\"\n        uid=1_1459 \" operators for mathematical addition, subtraction, and multiplication are similar to other languages, but the behavior of division differs. There are two types of division in Python: \"\n        uid=1_1460 link \"floor division\" description=\"Floor division\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Floor_division\"\n        uid=1_1462 \" (or integer division) \"\n        uid=1_1463 \"//\"\n        uid=1_1464 \", and floating-point division \"\n        uid=1_1465 \"/\"\n        uid=1_1466 \".\"\n        uid=1_1467 link \"[95]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-103\"\n        uid=1_1471 \" Python uses the \"\n        uid=1_1472 \"**\"\n        uid=1_1473 \" operator for exponentiation.\"\n        uid=1_1474 \"Python uses the \"\n        uid=1_1475 \"+\"\n        uid=1_1476 \" operator for string concatenation. The language uses the \"\n        uid=1_1477 \"*\"\n        uid=1_1478 \" operator for duplicating a string a specified number of times.\"\n        uid=1_1479 \"The \"\n        uid=1_1480 \"@\"\n        uid=1_1481 \" infix operator\"\n        uid=1_1482 \" is intended to be used by libraries such as \"\n        uid=1_1483 link \"NumPy\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/NumPy\"\n        uid=1_1485 \" for \"\n        uid=1_1486 link \"matrix multiplication\" description=\"Matrix multiplication\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Matrix_multiplication\"\n        uid=1_1488 \".\"\n        uid=1_1489 link \"[96]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-PEP465-104\"\n        uid=1_1493 link \"[97]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-Python3.5Changelog-105\"\n        uid=1_1497 \"The syntax \"\n        uid=1_1498 \":=\"\n        uid=1_1499 \", called the \"\"\n        uid=1_1500 \"walrus operator\"\n        uid=1_1501 \"\", was introduced in Python 3.8. This operator assigns values to variables as part of a larger expression.\"\n        uid=1_1502 link \"[98]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-Python3.8Changelog-106\"\n        uid=1_1506 \"In Python, \"\n        uid=1_1507 \"==\"\n        uid=1_1508 \" compares two objects by value. Python's \"\n        uid=1_1509 \"is\"\n        uid=1_1510 \" operator may be used to compare object identities (i.e., comparison by reference), and comparisons may be chained—for example, \"\n        uid=1_1511 \"a\"\n        uid=1_1512 \" \"\n        uid=1_1513 \"<=\"\n        uid=1_1514 \" \"\n        uid=1_1515 \"b\"\n        uid=1_1516 \" \"\n        uid=1_1517 \"<=\"\n        uid=1_1518 \" \"\n        uid=1_1519 \"c\"\n        uid=1_1520 \".\"\n        uid=1_1521 \"Python uses \"\n        uid=1_1522 \"and\"\n        uid=1_1523 \", \"\n        uid=1_1524 \"or\"\n        uid=1_1525 \", and \"\n        uid=1_1526 \"not\"\n        uid=1_1527 \" as Boolean operators.\"\n        uid=1_1528 \"Python has a type of expression called a \"\n        uid=1_1529 link \"list comprehension\" description=\"List comprehension\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_comprehension#Python\"\n        uid=1_1531 \", and a more general expression called a \"\n        uid=1_1532 \"generator expression\"\n        uid=1_1533 \".\"\n        uid=1_1534 link \"[65]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-AutoNT-59-68\"\n        uid=1_1538 link \"Anonymous functions\" description=\"Anonymous function\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anonymous_function\"\n        uid=1_1540 \" are implemented using \"\n        uid=1_1541 link \"lambda expressions\" description=\"Lambda (programming)\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lambda_(programming)\"\n        uid=1_1543 \"; however, there may be only one expression in each body.\"\n        uid=1_1544 \"Conditional expressions are written as \"\n        uid=1_1545 \"x\"\n        uid=1_1546 \" \"\n        uid=1_1547 \"if\"\n        uid=1_1548 \" \"\n        uid=1_1549 \"c\"\n        uid=1_1550 \" \"\n        uid=1_1551 \"else\"\n        uid=1_1552 \" \"\n        uid=1_1553 \"y\"\n        uid=1_1554 \".\"\n        uid=1_1555 link \"[99]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-AutoNT-60-107\"\n        uid=1_1559 \" (This is different in operand order from the \"\n        uid=1_1560 link \"c ? x : y\" description=\"?:\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/%3F:\"\n        uid=1_1566 \" operator common to many other languages.)\"\n        uid=1_1567 \"Python makes a distinction between \"\n        uid=1_1568 link \"lists\" description=\"List (computer science)\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_(computer_science)\"\n        uid=1_1570 \" and \"\n        uid=1_1571 link \"tuples\" description=\"Tuple\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tuple\"\n        uid=1_1573 \". Lists are written as \"\n        uid=1_1574 \"[\"\n        uid=1_1575 \"1\"\n        uid=1_1576 \",\"\n        uid=1_1577 \" \"\n        uid=1_1578 \"2\"\n        uid=1_1579 \",\"\n        uid=1_1580 \" \"\n        uid=1_1581 \"3\"\n        uid=1_1582 \"]\"\n        uid=1_1583 \", are mutable, and cannot be used as dictionary keys because they are not hashable. Tuples, written as \"\n        uid=1_1584 \"(\"\n        uid=1_1585 \"1\"\n        uid=1_1586 \",\"\n        uid=1_1587 \" \"\n        uid=1_1588 \"2\"\n        uid=1_1589 \",\"\n        uid=1_1590 \" \"\n        uid=1_1591 \"3\"\n        uid=1_1592 \")\"\n        uid=1_1593 \", are immutable and can be used as dictionary keys if all of their elements are hashable.\"\n        uid=1_1594 link \"[100]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-108\"\n        uid=1_1598 \" The \"\n        uid=1_1599 \"+\"\n        uid=1_1600 \" operator can be used to concatenate two tuples, which does not directly modify their contents, but produces a new tuple containing the elements of both. For example, given the variable \"\n        uid=1_1601 \"t\"\n        uid=1_1602 \" initially equal to \"\n        uid=1_1603 \"(\"\n        uid=1_1604 \"1\"\n        uid=1_1605 \",\"\n        uid=1_1606 \" \"\n        uid=1_1607 \"2\"\n        uid=1_1608 \",\"\n        uid=1_1609 \" \"\n        uid=1_1610 \"3\"\n        uid=1_1611 \")\"\n        uid=1_1612 \", executing \"\n        uid=1_1613 \"t\"\n        uid=1_1614 \" \"\n        uid=1_1615 \"=\"\n        uid=1_1616 \" \"\n        uid=1_1617 \"t\"\n        uid=1_1618 \" \"\n        uid=1_1619 \"+\"\n        uid=1_1620 \" \"\n        uid=1_1621 \"(\"\n        uid=1_1622 \"4\"\n        uid=1_1623 \",\"\n        uid=1_1624 \" \"\n        uid=1_1625 \"5\"\n        uid=1_1626 \")\"\n        uid=1_1627 \" first evaluates \"\n        uid=1_1628 \"t\"\n        uid=1_1629 \" \"\n        uid=1_1630 \"+\"\n        uid=1_1631 \" \"\n        uid=1_1632 \"(\"\n        uid=1_1633 \"4\"\n        uid=1_1634 \",\"\n        uid=1_1635 \" \"\n        uid=1_1636 \"5\"\n        uid=1_1637 \")\"\n        uid=1_1638 \", which yields \"\n        uid=1_1639 \"(\"\n        uid=1_1640 \"1\"\n        uid=1_1641 \",\"\n        uid=1_1642 \" \"\n        uid=1_1643 \"2\"\n        uid=1_1644 \",\"\n        uid=1_1645 \" \"\n        uid=1_1646 \"3\"\n        uid=1_1647 \",\"\n        uid=1_1648 \" \"\n        uid=1_1649 \"4\"\n        uid=1_1650 \",\"\n        uid=1_1651 \" \"\n        uid=1_1652 \"5\"\n        uid=1_1653 \")\"\n        uid=1_1654 \"; this result is then assigned back to \"\n        uid=1_1655 \"t\"\n        uid=1_1656 \"—thereby effectively \"modifying the contents\" of \"\n        uid=1_1657 \"t\"\n        uid=1_1658 \" while conforming to the immutable nature of tuple objects. Parentheses are optional for tuples in unambiguous contexts.\"\n        uid=1_1659 link \"[101]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-109\"\n        uid=1_1663 \"Python features \"\n        uid=1_1664 \"sequence unpacking\"\n        uid=1_1665 \" where multiple expressions, each evaluating to something assignable (e.g., a variable or a writable property) are associated just as in forming tuple literal; as a whole, the results are then put on the left-hand side of the equal sign in an assignment statement. This statement expects an \"\n        uid=1_1666 \"iterable\"\n        uid=1_1667 \" object on the right-hand side of the equal sign to produce the same number of values as the writable expressions on the left-hand side; while iterating, the statement assigns each of the values produced on the right to the corresponding expression on the left.\"\n        uid=1_1668 link \"[102]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-110\"\n        uid=1_1672 \"Python has a \"string format\" operator \"\n        uid=1_1673 \"%\"\n        uid=1_1674 \" that functions analogously to \"\n        uid=1_1675 link \"printf\" description=\"Printf\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Printf\"\n        uid=1_1677 \" format strings in the C language—e.g. \"\n        uid=1_1678 \"\"spam=\"\n        uid=1_1679 \"%s\"\n        uid=1_1680 \" eggs=\"\n        uid=1_1681 \"%d\"\n        uid=1_1682 \"\"\"\n        uid=1_1683 \" \"\n        uid=1_1684 \"%\"\n        uid=1_1685 \" \"\n        uid=1_1686 \"(\"\n        uid=1_1687 \"\"blah\"\"\n        uid=1_1688 \",\"\n        uid=1_1689 \" \"\n        uid=1_1690 \"2\"\n        uid=1_1691 \")\"\n        uid=1_1692 \" evaluates to \"\n        uid=1_1693 \"\"spam=blah eggs=2\"\"\n        uid=1_1694 \". In Python\"\n        uid=1_1695 \" \"\n        uid=1_1696 \"2.6+ and 3+, this operator was supplemented by the \"\n        uid=1_1697 \"format()\"\n        uid=1_1698 \" method of the \"\n        uid=1_1699 \"str\"\n        uid=1_1700 \" class, e.g., \"\n        uid=1_1701 \"\"spam=\"\n        uid=1_1702 \"{0}\"\n        uid=1_1703 \" eggs=\"\n        uid=1_1704 \"{1}\"\n        uid=1_1705 \"\"\"\n        uid=1_1706 \".\"\n        uid=1_1707 \"format\"\n        uid=1_1708 \"(\"\n        uid=1_1709 \"\"blah\"\"\n        uid=1_1710 \",\"\n        uid=1_1711 \" \"\n        uid=1_1712 \"2\"\n        uid=1_1713 \")\"\n        uid=1_1714 \". Python\"\n        uid=1_1715 \" \"\n        uid=1_1716 \"3.6 added \"f-strings\": \"\n        uid=1_1717 \"spam\"\n        uid=1_1718 \" \"\n        uid=1_1719 \"=\"\n        uid=1_1720 \" \"\n        uid=1_1721 \"\"blah\"\"\n        uid=1_1722 \";\"\n        uid=1_1723 \" \"\n        uid=1_1724 \"eggs\"\n        uid=1_1725 \" \"\n        uid=1_1726 \"=\"\n        uid=1_1727 \" \"\n        uid=1_1728 \"2\"\n        uid=1_1729 \";\"\n        uid=1_1730 \" \"\n        uid=1_1731 \"f\"\n        uid=1_1732 \"'spam=\"\n        uid=1_1733 \"{\"\n        uid=1_1734 \"spam\"\n        uid=1_1735 \"}\"\n        uid=1_1736 \" eggs=\"\n        uid=1_1737 \"{\"\n        uid=1_1738 \"eggs\"\n        uid=1_1739 \"}\"\n        uid=1_1740 \"'\"\n        uid=1_1741 \".\"\n        uid=1_1742 link \"[103]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-pep-0498-111\"\n        uid=1_1746 \"Strings in Python can be \"\n        uid=1_1747 link \"concatenated\" description=\"Concatenated\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Concatenated\"\n        uid=1_1749 \" by \"adding\" them (using the same operator as for adding integers and floats); e.g., \"\n        uid=1_1750 \"\"spam\"\"\n        uid=1_1751 \" \"\n        uid=1_1752 \"+\"\n        uid=1_1753 \" \"\n        uid=1_1754 \"\"eggs\"\"\n        uid=1_1755 \" returns \"\n        uid=1_1756 \"\"spameggs\"\"\n        uid=1_1757 \". If strings contain numbers, they are concatenated as strings rather than as integers, e.g. \"\n        uid=1_1758 \"\"2\"\"\n        uid=1_1759 \" \"\n        uid=1_1760 \"+\"\n        uid=1_1761 \" \"\n        uid=1_1762 \"\"2\"\"\n        uid=1_1763 \" returns \"\n        uid=1_1764 \"\"22\"\"\n        uid=1_1765 \".\"\n        uid=1_1766 \"Python supports \"\n        uid=1_1767 link \"string literals\" description=\"String literal\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/String_literal\"\n        uid=1_1769 \" in several ways:\"\n        uid=1_1770 \"Delimited by single or double quotation marks; single and double quotation marks have equivalent functionality (unlike in \"\n        uid=1_1771 link \"Unix shells\" description=\"Unix shell\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Unix_shell\"\n        uid=1_1773 \", \"\n        uid=1_1774 link \"Perl\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Perl\"\n        uid=1_1776 \", and Perl-influenced languages). Both marks use the backslash (\"\n        uid=1_1777 \"\\\"\n        uid=1_1778 \") as an \"\n        uid=1_1779 link \"escape character\" description=\"Escape character\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Escape_character\"\n        uid=1_1781 \". \"\n        uid=1_1782 link \"String interpolation\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/String_interpolation\"\n        uid=1_1784 \" became available in Python\"\n        uid=1_1785 \" \"\n        uid=1_1786 \"3.6 as \"formatted string literals\".\"\n        uid=1_1787 link \"[103]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-pep-0498-111\"\n        uid=1_1791 \"Triple-quoted, i.e., starting and ending with three single or double quotation marks; this may span multiple lines and function like \"\n        uid=1_1792 link \"here documents\" description=\"Here document\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Here_document\"\n        uid=1_1794 \" in shells, Perl, and \"\n        uid=1_1795 link \"Ruby\" description=\"Ruby (programming language)\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ruby_(programming_language)\"\n        uid=1_1797 \".\"\n        uid=1_1798 link \"Raw string\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Raw_string\"\n        uid=1_1800 \" varieties, denoted by prefixing the string literal with \"\n        uid=1_1801 \"r\"\n        uid=1_1802 \". Escape sequences are not interpreted; hence raw strings are useful where literal backslashes are common, such as in \"\n        uid=1_1803 link \"regular expressions\" description=\"Regular expression\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Regular_expression\"\n        uid=1_1805 \" and \"\n        uid=1_1806 link \"Windows\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Windows\"\n        uid=1_1808 \"-style paths. (Compare \"\"\n        uid=1_1809 \"@\"\n        uid=1_1810 \"-quoting\" in \"\n        uid=1_1811 link \"C#\" description=\"C Sharp (programming language)\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/C_Sharp_(programming_language)\"\n        uid=1_1813 \".)\"\n        uid=1_1814 \"Python has \"\n        uid=1_1815 link \"array index\" description=\"Array index\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Array_index\"\n        uid=1_1817 \" and \"\n        uid=1_1818 link \"array slicing\" description=\"Array slicing\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Array_slicing\"\n        uid=1_1820 \" expressions in lists, which are written as \"\n        uid=1_1821 \"a[key]\"\n        uid=1_1822 \", \"\n        uid=1_1823 \"a\"\n        uid=1_1824 \"[\"\n        uid=1_1825 \"start\"\n        uid=1_1826 \":\"\n        uid=1_1827 \"stop\"\n        uid=1_1828 \"]\"\n        uid=1_1829 \" or \"\n        uid=1_1830 \"a\"\n        uid=1_1831 \"[\"\n        uid=1_1832 \"start\"\n        uid=1_1833 \":\"\n        uid=1_1834 \"stop\"\n        uid=1_1835 \":\"\n        uid=1_1836 \"step\"\n        uid=1_1837 \"]\"\n        uid=1_1838 \". Indexes are \"\n        uid=1_1839 link \"zero-based\" description=\"Zero-based\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zero-based\"\n        uid=1_1841 \", and negative indexes are relative to the end. Slices take elements from the \"\n        uid=1_1842 \"start\"\n        uid=1_1843 \" index up to, but not including, the \"\n        uid=1_1844 \"stop\"\n        uid=1_1845 \" index. The (optional) third slice \"\n        uid=1_1846 link \"parameter\" description=\"Parameter (computer programming)\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Parameter_(computer_programming)\"\n        uid=1_1848 \", called \"\n        uid=1_1849 \"step\"\n        uid=1_1850 \" or \"\n        uid=1_1851 \"stride\"\n        uid=1_1852 \", allows elements to be skipped or reversed. Slice indexes may be omitted—for example, \"\n        uid=1_1853 \"a\"\n        uid=1_1854 \"[:]\"\n        uid=1_1855 \" returns a copy of the entire list. Each element of a slice is a \"\n        uid=1_1856 link \"shallow copy\" description=\"Shallow copy\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shallow_copy\"\n        uid=1_1858 \".\"\n        uid=1_1859 \"In Python, a distinction between expressions and statements is rigidly enforced, in contrast to languages such as \"\n        uid=1_1860 link \"Common Lisp\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Common_Lisp\"\n        uid=1_1862 \", \"\n        uid=1_1863 link \"Scheme\" description=\"Scheme (programming language)\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Scheme_(programming_language)\"\n        uid=1_1865 \", or \"\n        uid=1_1866 link \"Ruby\" description=\"Ruby (programming language)\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ruby_(programming_language)\"\n        uid=1_1868 \". This distinction leads to duplicating some functionality, for example:\"\n        uid=1_1869 link \"List comprehensions\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_comprehensions\"\n        uid=1_1871 \" vs. \"\n        uid=1_1872 \"for\"\n        uid=1_1873 \"-loops\"\n        uid=1_1874 link \"Conditional\" description=\"Conditional (computer programming)\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Conditional_(computer_programming)\"\n        uid=1_1876 \" expressions vs. \"\n        uid=1_1877 \"if\"\n        uid=1_1878 \" blocks\"\n        uid=1_1879 \"The \"\n        uid=1_1880 \"eval()\"\n        uid=1_1881 \" vs. \"\n        uid=1_1882 \"exec()\"\n        uid=1_1883 \" built-in functions (in Python\"\n        uid=1_1884 \" \"\n        uid=1_1885 \"2, \"\n        uid=1_1886 \"exec\"\n        uid=1_1887 \" is a statement); the former function is for expressions, while the latter is for statements\"\n        uid=1_1888 \"A statement cannot be part of an expression; because of this restriction, expressions such as list and \"\n        uid=1_1889 \"dict\"\n        uid=1_1890 \" comprehensions (and lambda expressions) cannot contain statements. As a particular case, an assignment statement such as \"\n        uid=1_1891 \"a\"\n        uid=1_1892 \" \"\n        uid=1_1893 \"=\"\n        uid=1_1894 \" \"\n        uid=1_1895 \"1\"\n        uid=1_1896 \" cannot be part of the conditional expression of a conditional statement.\"\n      uid=1_1897 region \"Typing\"\n        uid=1_1898 heading \"Typing\" level=\"3\"\n        uid=1_1899 link \"edit\" description=\"Edit section: Typing\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Python_(programming_language)&action=edit&section=8\"\n        uid=1_1901 link url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Python_3.13_Standrd_Type_Hierarchy-en.svg\"\n        uid=1_1902 \"The standard type hierarchy in Python\"\n        uid=1_1903 \" \"\n        uid=1_1904 \"3\"\n        uid=1_1905 \"Python uses \"\n        uid=1_1906 link \"duck typing\" description=\"Duck typing\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Duck_typing\"\n        uid=1_1908 \", and it has typed objects but untyped variable names. Type constraints are not checked at definition time; rather, operations on an object may fail at usage time, indicating that the object is not of an appropriate type. Despite being \"\n        uid=1_1909 link \"dynamically typed\" description=\"Dynamically typed\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dynamically_typed\"\n        uid=1_1911 \", Python is \"\n        uid=1_1912 link \"strongly typed\" description=\"Strongly typed\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Strongly_typed\"\n        uid=1_1914 \", forbidding operations that are poorly defined (e.g., adding a number and a string) rather than quietly attempting to interpret them.\"\n        uid=1_1915 \"Python allows programmers to define their own types using \"\n        uid=1_1916 link \"classes\" description=\"Class (computer science)\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Class_(computer_science)\"\n        uid=1_1918 \", most often for \"\n        uid=1_1919 link \"object-oriented programming\" description=\"Object-oriented programming\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Object-oriented_programming\"\n        uid=1_1921 \". New \"\n        uid=1_1922 link \"instances\" description=\"Object (computer science)\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Object_(computer_science)\"\n        uid=1_1924 \" of classes are constructed by calling the class, for example, \"\n        uid=1_1925 \"SpamClass\"\n        uid=1_1926 \"()\"\n        uid=1_1927 \" or \"\n        uid=1_1928 \"EggsClass\"\n        uid=1_1929 \"()\"\n        uid=1_1930 \"); the classes are instances of the \"\n        uid=1_1931 link \"metaclass\" description=\"Metaclass\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Metaclass\"\n        uid=1_1933 \" \"\n        uid=1_1934 \"type\"\n        uid=1_1935 \" (which is an instance of itself), thereby allowing metaprogramming and \"\n        uid=1_1936 link \"reflection\" description=\"Reflective programming\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Reflective_programming\"\n        uid=1_1938 \".\"\n        uid=1_1939 \"Before version 3.0, Python had two kinds of classes, both using the same syntax: \"\n        uid=1_1940 \"old-style\"\n        uid=1_1941 \" and \"\n        uid=1_1942 \"new-style\"\n        uid=1_1943 \".\"\n        uid=1_1944 link \"[104]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-classy-112\"\n        uid=1_1948 \" Current Python versions support the semantics of only the new style.\"\n        uid=1_1949 \"Python supports \"\n        uid=1_1950 link \"optional type annotations\" description=\"Optional typing\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Optional_typing\"\n        uid=1_1952 \".\"\n        uid=1_1953 link \"[5]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-type_hint-PEP-5\"\n        uid=1_1957 link \"[105]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-113\"\n        uid=1_1961 \" These annotations are not enforced by the language, but may be used by external tools such as \"\n        uid=1_1962 \"mypy\"\n        uid=1_1963 \" to catch errors. Python includes a module \"\n        uid=1_1964 \"typing\"\n        uid=1_1965 \" including several type names for type annotations.\"\n        uid=1_1966 link \"[106]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-114\"\n        uid=1_1970 link \"[107]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-115\"\n        uid=1_1974 \" Also, mypy supports a Python compiler called mypyc, which leverages type annotations for optimization.\"\n        uid=1_1975 link \"[108]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-116\"\n        uid=1_1979 \"Summary of Python 3's built-in types\"\n        uid=1_1980 \"Type\"\n        uid=1_1981 link \"Mutability\" description=\"Immutable object\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Immutable_object\"\n        uid=1_1983 \"Description\"\n        uid=1_1984 \"Syntax examples\"\n        uid=1_1985 \"bool\"\n        uid=1_1986 \"immutable\"\n        uid=1_1987 link \"Boolean value\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Boolean_value\"\n        uid=1_1989 \"True\"\n        uid=1_1990 LineBreak \"\n\"\n        uid=1_1991 \"False\"\n        uid=1_1992 \"bytearray\"\n        uid=1_1993 \"mutable\"\n        uid=1_1994 \"Sequence of \"\n        uid=1_1995 link \"bytes\" description=\"Byte\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Byte\"\n        uid=1_1997 \"bytearray\"\n        uid=1_1998 \"(\"\n        uid=1_1999 \"b\"\n        uid=1_2000 \"'Some ASCII'\"\n        uid=1_2001 \")\"\n        uid=1_2002 LineBreak \"\n\"\n        uid=1_2003 \"bytearray\"\n        uid=1_2004 \"(\"\n        uid=1_2005 \"b\"\n        uid=1_2006 \"\"Some ASCII\"\"\n        uid=1_2007 \")\"\n        uid=1_2008 LineBreak \"\n\"\n        uid=1_2009 \"bytearray\"\n        uid=1_2010 \"([\"\n        uid=1_2011 \"119\"\n        uid=1_2012 \",\"\n        uid=1_2013 \" \"\n        uid=1_2014 \"105\"\n        uid=1_2015 \",\"\n        uid=1_2016 \" \"\n        uid=1_2017 \"107\"\n        uid=1_2018 \",\"\n        uid=1_2019 \" \"\n        uid=1_2020 \"105\"\n        uid=1_2021 \"])\"\n        uid=1_2022 \"bytes\"\n        uid=1_2023 \"immutable\"\n        uid=1_2024 \"Sequence of bytes\"\n        uid=1_2025 \"b\"\n        uid=1_2026 \"'Some ASCII'\"\n        uid=1_2027 LineBreak \"\n\"\n        uid=1_2028 \"b\"\n        uid=1_2029 \"\"Some ASCII\"\"\n        uid=1_2030 LineBreak \"\n\"\n        uid=1_2031 \"bytes\"\n        uid=1_2032 \"([\"\n        uid=1_2033 \"119\"\n        uid=1_2034 \",\"\n        uid=1_2035 \" \"\n        uid=1_2036 \"105\"\n        uid=1_2037 \",\"\n        uid=1_2038 \" \"\n        uid=1_2039 \"107\"\n        uid=1_2040 \",\"\n        uid=1_2041 \" \"\n        uid=1_2042 \"105\"\n        uid=1_2043 \"])\"\n        uid=1_2044 \"complex\"\n        uid=1_2045 \"immutable\"\n        uid=1_2046 link \"Complex number\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Complex_number\"\n        uid=1_2048 \" with real and imaginary parts\"\n        uid=1_2049 \"3\"\n        uid=1_2050 \"+\"\n        uid=1_2051 \"2.7\"\n        uid=1_2052 \"j\"\n        uid=1_2053 LineBreak \"\n\"\n        uid=1_2054 \"3\"\n        uid=1_2055 \" \"\n        uid=1_2056 \"+\"\n        uid=1_2057 \" \"\n        uid=1_2058 \"2.7\"\n        uid=1_2059 \"j\"\n        uid=1_2060 LineBreak \"\n\"\n        uid=1_2061 \"5\"\n        uid=1_2062 \"j\"\n        uid=1_2063 \"dict\"\n        uid=1_2064 \"mutable\"\n        uid=1_2065 link \"Associative array\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Associative_array\"\n        uid=1_2067 \" (or dictionary) of key and value pairs; can contain mixed types (keys and values); keys must be a hashable type\"\n        uid=1_2068 \"{\"\n        uid=1_2069 \"'key1'\"\n        uid=1_2070 \":\"\n        uid=1_2071 \" \"\n        uid=1_2072 \"1.0\"\n        uid=1_2073 \",\"\n        uid=1_2074 \" \"\n        uid=1_2075 \"3\"\n        uid=1_2076 \":\"\n        uid=1_2077 \" \"\n        uid=1_2078 \"False\"\n        uid=1_2079 \"}\"\n        uid=1_2080 LineBreak \"\n\"\n        uid=1_2081 \"{}\"\n        uid=1_2082 \"frozendict\"\n        uid=1_2083 \"immutable\"\n        uid=1_2084 \"Same as dict, but read-only\"\n        uid=1_2085 \"frozendict(a=1,b=2)\"\n        uid=1_2086 \"types.EllipsisType\"\n        uid=1_2087 \"immutable\"\n        uid=1_2088 \"An \"\n        uid=1_2089 link \"ellipsis\" description=\"Ellipsis (programming operator)\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ellipsis_(programming_operator)\"\n        uid=1_2091 \" placeholder to be used as an index in \"\n        uid=1_2092 link \"NumPy\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/NumPy\"\n        uid=1_2094 \" arrays\"\n        uid=1_2095 \"...\"\n        uid=1_2096 LineBreak \"\n\"\n        uid=1_2097 \"Ellipsis\"\n        uid=1_2098 \"float\"\n        uid=1_2099 \"immutable\"\n        uid=1_2100 \"64-bit \"\n        uid=1_2101 link \"double-precision\" description=\"Double-precision\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Double-precision\"\n        uid=1_2103 \" \"\n        uid=1_2104 link \"floating-point number\" description=\"Floating-point number\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Floating-point_number\"\n        uid=1_2106 \" (\"\n        uid=1_2107 link \"IEEE 754\" description=\"IEEE 754\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/IEEE_754\"\n        uid=1_2109 \" number with 53\"\n        uid=1_2110 \" \"\n        uid=1_2111 \"bits of precision, in all supported versions since CPython 3.11.\"\n        uid=1_2112 link \"[109]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-117\"\n        uid=1_2116 \"Python's built-in type \"\n        uid=1_2117 \"memoryview\"\n        uid=1_2118 \" does though support both 64-bit doubles and 32-bit floats, and Python's standard library \"\n        uid=1_2119 \"struct\"\n        uid=1_2120 \" module additionally supports 16-bit half-floats. Python packages like NumPy and Pandas may also support 32-bit floats or more, e.g. \"\n        uid=1_2121 \"numpy.half\"\n        uid=1_2122 \"; though support for half-floats is often incomplete or non-existent in most packages.\"\n        uid=1_2123 link \"[110]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-118\"\n        uid=1_2127 \" \"\n        uid=1_2128 \"16-bit \"\n        uid=1_2129 link \"bfloat\" description=\"Bfloat16 floating-point format\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bfloat16_floating-point_format\"\n        uid=1_2131 \" has support in a few packages.\"\n        uid=1_2132 link \"[111]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-119\"\n        uid=1_2136 \"Most Python implementations choose to support the double kind, but some unusual (subset) implementations, such as \"\n        uid=1_2137 link \"MicroPython\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/MicroPython\"\n        uid=1_2139 \" for \"\n        uid=1_2140 link \"embedded programming\" description=\"Embedded programming\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Embedded_programming\"\n        uid=1_2142 \", support 32-bit IEEE floats as their default float. Users can opt into 64-bit by setting MICROPY_FLOAT_IMPL to MICROPY_FLOAT_IMPL_DOUBLE.\"\n        uid=1_2143 \"1.33333\"\n        uid=1_2144 \"frozenset\"\n        uid=1_2145 \"immutable\"\n        uid=1_2146 \"Unordered \"\n        uid=1_2147 link \"set\" description=\"Set (computer science)\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Set_(computer_science)\"\n        uid=1_2149 \", contains no duplicates; can contain mixed types, if hashable\"\n        uid=1_2150 \"frozenset\"\n        uid=1_2151 \"({\"\n        uid=1_2152 \"4.0\"\n        uid=1_2153 \",\"\n        uid=1_2154 \" \"\n        uid=1_2155 \"'string'\"\n        uid=1_2156 \",\"\n        uid=1_2157 \" \"\n        uid=1_2158 \"True\"\n        uid=1_2159 \"})\"\n        uid=1_2160 \"frozenset\"\n        uid=1_2161 \"()\"\n        uid=1_2162 \"int\"\n        uid=1_2163 \"immutable\"\n        uid=1_2164 link \"Integer\" description=\"Integer (computer science)\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Integer_(computer_science)\"\n        uid=1_2166 \" of unlimited magnitude\"\n        uid=1_2167 link \"[112]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-pep0237-120\"\n        uid=1_2171 \" (i.e. not using machine integers; e.g. the Python package NumPy uses fixed-sized integers for speedup (and allows different sizes), such as types \"\n        uid=1_2172 \"numpy.byte\"\n        uid=1_2173 \" and \"\n        uid=1_2174 \"numpy.ulonglong\"\n        uid=1_2175 \", all such types have a possibility of wrap-around, though less likely the larger it is)\"\n        uid=1_2176 \"42\"\n        uid=1_2177 \"list\"\n        uid=1_2178 \"mutable\"\n        uid=1_2179 link \"List\" description=\"List (computer science)\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_(computer_science)\"\n        uid=1_2181 \", can contain mixed types\"\n        uid=1_2182 \"[\"\n        uid=1_2183 \"4.0\"\n        uid=1_2184 \",\"\n        uid=1_2185 \" \"\n        uid=1_2186 \"'string'\"\n        uid=1_2187 \",\"\n        uid=1_2188 \" \"\n        uid=1_2189 \"True\"\n        uid=1_2190 \"]\"\n        uid=1_2191 LineBreak \"\n\"\n        uid=1_2192 \"[]\"\n        uid=1_2193 \"types.NoneType\"\n        uid=1_2194 \"immutable\"\n        uid=1_2195 \"An object representing the absence of a value, often called \"\n        uid=1_2196 link \"null\" description=\"Null pointer\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Null_pointer\"\n        uid=1_2198 \" in other languages\"\n        uid=1_2199 \"None\"\n        uid=1_2200 \"types.NotImplementedType\"\n        uid=1_2201 \"immutable\"\n        uid=1_2202 \"A placeholder that can be returned from \"\n        uid=1_2203 link \"overloaded operators\" description=\"Operator overloading\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operator_overloading\"\n        uid=1_2205 \" to indicate unsupported operand types\"\n        uid=1_2206 \"NotImplemented\"\n        uid=1_2207 \"range\"\n        uid=1_2208 \"immutable\"\n        uid=1_2209 \"An \"\n        uid=1_2210 \"immutable sequence\"\n        uid=1_2211 \" of numbers, commonly used for iterating a specific number of times in \"\n        uid=1_2212 \"for\"\n        uid=1_2213 \" loops\"\n        uid=1_2214 link \"[113]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-121\"\n        uid=1_2218 \"range\"\n        uid=1_2219 \"(\"\n        uid=1_2220 \"−\"\n        uid=1_2221 \"1\"\n        uid=1_2222 \",\"\n        uid=1_2223 \" \"\n        uid=1_2224 \"10\"\n        uid=1_2225 \")\"\n        uid=1_2226 LineBreak \"\n\"\n        uid=1_2227 \"range\"\n        uid=1_2228 \"(\"\n        uid=1_2229 \"10\"\n        uid=1_2230 \",\"\n        uid=1_2231 \" \"\n        uid=1_2232 \"−\"\n        uid=1_2233 \"5\"\n        uid=1_2234 \",\"\n        uid=1_2235 \" \"\n        uid=1_2236 \"−\"\n        uid=1_2237 \"2\"\n        uid=1_2238 \")\"\n        uid=1_2239 \"set\"\n        uid=1_2240 \"mutable\"\n        uid=1_2241 \"Unordered \"\n        uid=1_2242 link \"set\" description=\"Set (computer science)\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Set_(computer_science)\"\n        uid=1_2244 \", contains no duplicates; can contain mixed types, if hashable\"\n        uid=1_2245 \"{\"\n        uid=1_2246 \"4.0\"\n        uid=1_2247 \",\"\n        uid=1_2248 \" \"\n        uid=1_2249 \"'string'\"\n        uid=1_2250 \",\"\n        uid=1_2251 \" \"\n        uid=1_2252 \"True\"\n        uid=1_2253 \"}\"\n        uid=1_2254 LineBreak \"\n\"\n        uid=1_2255 \"set\"\n        uid=1_2256 \"()\"\n        uid=1_2257 \"str\"\n        uid=1_2258 \"immutable\"\n        uid=1_2259 \"A \"\n        uid=1_2260 link \"character string\" description=\"Character string\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Character_string\"\n        uid=1_2262 \": sequence of Unicode codepoints\"\n        uid=1_2263 \"'Wikipedia'\"\n        uid=1_2264 LineBreak \"\n\"\n        uid=1_2265 \"\"Wikipedia\"\"\n        uid=1_2266 \"\"\"\"Spanning\"\n        uid=1_2267 \"\n\"\n        uid=1_2268 \"multiple\"\n        uid=1_2269 \"\n\"\n        uid=1_2270 \"lines\"\"\"\"\n        uid=1_2271 \"\n\"\n        uid=1_2272 \"tuple\"\n        uid=1_2273 \"immutable\"\n        uid=1_2274 link \"Tuple\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tuple\"\n        uid=1_2276 \", can contain mixed types\"\n        uid=1_2277 \"(\"\n        uid=1_2278 \"4.0\"\n        uid=1_2279 \",\"\n        uid=1_2280 \" \"\n        uid=1_2281 \"'string'\"\n        uid=1_2282 \",\"\n        uid=1_2283 \" \"\n        uid=1_2284 \"True\"\n        uid=1_2285 \")\"\n        uid=1_2286 LineBreak \"\n\"\n        uid=1_2287 \"(\"\n        uid=1_2288 \"'single element'\"\n        uid=1_2289 \",)\"\n        uid=1_2290 LineBreak \"\n\"\n        uid=1_2291 \"()\"\n      uid=1_2292 region \"Arithmetic operations\"\n        uid=1_2293 heading \"Arithmetic operations\" level=\"3\"\n        uid=1_2294 link \"edit\" description=\"Edit section: Arithmetic operations\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Python_(programming_language)&action=edit&section=9\"\n        uid=1_2296 \"Python includes conventional symbols for arithmetic operators (\"\n        uid=1_2297 \"+\"\n        uid=1_2298 \", \"\n        uid=1_2299 \"-\"\n        uid=1_2300 \", \"\n        uid=1_2301 \"*\"\n        uid=1_2302 \", \"\n        uid=1_2303 \"/\"\n        uid=1_2304 \"), the floor-division operator \"\n        uid=1_2305 \"//\"\n        uid=1_2306 \", and the \"\n        uid=1_2307 link \"modulo operator\" description=\"Modulo operator\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Modulo_operator\"\n        uid=1_2309 \" \"\n        uid=1_2310 \"%\"\n        uid=1_2311 \". (With the modulo operator, a remainder can be negative,\"\n        uid=1_2312 \" e.g., \"\n        uid=1_2313 \"4\"\n        uid=1_2314 \" \"\n        uid=1_2315 \"% -3 == -2\"\n        uid=1_2316 \".) Python also offers the \"\n        uid=1_2317 \"**\"\n        uid=1_2318 \" symbol for \"\n        uid=1_2319 link \"exponentiation\" description=\"Exponentiation\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Exponentiation\"\n        uid=1_2321 \", e.g. \"\n        uid=1_2322 \"5**3 == 125\"\n        uid=1_2323 \" and \"\n        uid=1_2324 \"9**0.5 == 3.0\"\n        uid=1_2325 \", as well as the matrix‑multiplication operator \"\n        uid=1_2326 \"@\"\n        uid=1_2327 \".\"\n        uid=1_2328 link \"[114]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-122\"\n        uid=1_2332 \" These operators work as in traditional mathematics; with the same \"\n        uid=1_2333 link \"precedence rules\" description=\"Order of operations\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Order_of_operations\"\n        uid=1_2335 \", the \"\n        uid=1_2336 link \"infix\" description=\"Infix notation\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Infix_notation\"\n        uid=1_2338 \" operators \"\n        uid=1_2339 \"+\"\n        uid=1_2340 \" and \"\n        uid=1_2341 \"-\"\n        uid=1_2342 \" can also be \"\n        uid=1_2343 link \"unary\" description=\"Unary operation\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Unary_operation\"\n        uid=1_2345 \", to represent positive and negative numbers respectively.\"\n        uid=1_2346 \"Division between integers produces floating-point results. The behavior of division has changed significantly over time:\"\n        uid=1_2347 link \"[115]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-pep0238-123\"\n        uid=1_2351 \"The current version of Python (i.e., since 3.0) changed the \"\n        uid=1_2352 \"/\"\n        uid=1_2353 \" operator to always represent floating-point division, e.g., \"\n        uid=1_2354 \"5\"\n        uid=1_2355 \"/\"\n        uid=1_2356 \"2\"\n        uid=1_2357 \" \"\n        uid=1_2358 \"==\"\n        uid=1_2359 \" \"\n        uid=1_2360 \"2.5\"\n        uid=1_2361 \".\"\n        uid=1_2362 \"The floor division \"\n        uid=1_2363 \"//\"\n        uid=1_2364 \" operator was introduced, meaning that \"\n        uid=1_2365 \"7//3 == 2\"\n        uid=1_2366 \", \"\n        uid=1_2367 \"-7//3 == -3\"\n        uid=1_2368 \", \"\n        uid=1_2369 \"7.5//3 == 2.0\"\n        uid=1_2370 \", and \"\n        uid=1_2371 \"-7.5//3 == -3.0\"\n        uid=1_2372 \". For Python 2.7, adding the \"\n        uid=1_2373 \"from\"\n        uid=1_2374 \" \"\n        uid=1_2375 \"__future__\"\n        uid=1_2376 \" \"\n        uid=1_2377 \"import\"\n        uid=1_2378 \" \"\n        uid=1_2379 \"division\"\n        uid=1_2380 \" statement allows a module in Python 2.7 to use Python 3.x rules for division (see above).\"\n        uid=1_2381 \"In Python terms, the \"\n        uid=1_2382 \"/\"\n        uid=1_2383 \" operator represents \"\n        uid=1_2384 \"true division\"\n        uid=1_2385 \" (or simply \"\n        uid=1_2386 \"division\"\n        uid=1_2387 \"), while the \"\n        uid=1_2388 \"//\"\n        uid=1_2389 \" operator represents \"\n        uid=1_2390 \"floor division.\"\n        uid=1_2391 \" Before version 3.0, the \"\n        uid=1_2392 \"/\"\n        uid=1_2393 \" operator represents \"\n        uid=1_2394 \"classic division\"\n        uid=1_2395 \".\"\n        uid=1_2396 link \"[115]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-pep0238-123\"\n        uid=1_2400 link \"Rounding\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rounding\"\n        uid=1_2402 \" towards negative infinity, though a different method than in most languages, adds consistency to Python. For instance, this rounding implies that the equation \"\n        uid=1_2403 \"(\"\n        uid=1_2404 \"a\"\n        uid=1_2405 \" \"\n        uid=1_2406 \"+\"\n        uid=1_2407 \" \"\n        uid=1_2408 \"b\"\n        uid=1_2409 \")\"\n        uid=1_2410 \"//\"\n        uid=1_2411 \"b\"\n        uid=1_2412 \" \"\n        uid=1_2413 \"==\"\n        uid=1_2414 \" \"\n        uid=1_2415 \"a\"\n        uid=1_2416 \"//\"\n        uid=1_2417 \"b\"\n        uid=1_2418 \" \"\n        uid=1_2419 \"+\"\n        uid=1_2420 \" \"\n        uid=1_2421 \"1\"\n        uid=1_2422 \" is always true. Also, the rounding implies that the equation \"\n        uid=1_2423 \"b\"\n        uid=1_2424 \"*\"\n        uid=1_2425 \"(\"\n        uid=1_2426 \"a\"\n        uid=1_2427 \"//\"\n        uid=1_2428 \"b\"\n        uid=1_2429 \")\"\n        uid=1_2430 \" \"\n        uid=1_2431 \"+\"\n        uid=1_2432 \" \"\n        uid=1_2433 \"a\"\n        uid=1_2434 \"%\"\n        uid=1_2435 \"b\"\n        uid=1_2436 \" \"\n        uid=1_2437 \"==\"\n        uid=1_2438 \" \"\n        uid=1_2439 \"a\"\n        uid=1_2440 \" is valid for both positive and negative values of \"\n        uid=1_2441 \"a\"\n        uid=1_2442 \". As expected, the result of \"\n        uid=1_2443 \"a%b\"\n        uid=1_2444 \" lies in the \"\n        uid=1_2445 link \"half-open interval\" description=\"Half-open interval\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Half-open_interval\"\n        uid=1_2447 \" [0, \"\n        uid=1_2448 \"b\"\n        uid=1_2449 \"), where \"\n        uid=1_2450 \"b\"\n        uid=1_2451 \" is a positive integer; however, maintaining the validity of the equation requires that the result must lie in the interval (\"\n        uid=1_2452 \"b\"\n        uid=1_2453 \", 0] when \"\n        uid=1_2454 \"b\"\n        uid=1_2455 \" is negative.\"\n        uid=1_2456 link \"[116]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-AutoNT-62-124\"\n        uid=1_2460 \"Python provides a \"\n        uid=1_2461 \"round\"\n        uid=1_2462 \" function for rounding a float to the nearest integer. For \"\n        uid=1_2463 link \"tie-breaking\" description=\"Rounding\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rounding#Tie-breaking\"\n        uid=1_2465 \", Python\"\n        uid=1_2466 \" \"\n        uid=1_2467 \"3 uses the \"\n        uid=1_2468 \"round to even\"\n        uid=1_2469 \" method: \"\n        uid=1_2470 \"round(1.5)\"\n        uid=1_2471 \" and \"\n        uid=1_2472 \"round(2.5)\"\n        uid=1_2473 \" both produce \"\n        uid=1_2474 \"2\"\n        uid=1_2475 \".\"\n        uid=1_2476 link \"[117]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-AutoNT-64-125\"\n        uid=1_2480 \" Python versions before 3 used the \"\n        uid=1_2481 link \"round-away-from-zero\" description=\"Rounding\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rounding#Rounding_away_from_zero\"\n        uid=1_2483 \" method: \"\n        uid=1_2484 \"round(0.5)\"\n        uid=1_2485 \" is \"\n        uid=1_2486 \"1.0\"\n        uid=1_2487 \", and \"\n        uid=1_2488 \"round(-0.5)\"\n        uid=1_2489 \" is \"\n        uid=1_2490 \"−1.0\"\n        uid=1_2491 \".\"\n        uid=1_2492 link \"[118]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-AutoNT-63-126\"\n        uid=1_2496 \"Python allows Boolean expressions that contain multiple equality relations to be consistent with general usage in mathematics. For example, the expression \"\n        uid=1_2497 \"a < b < c\"\n        uid=1_2498 \" tests whether \"\n        uid=1_2499 \"a\"\n        uid=1_2500 \" is less than \"\n        uid=1_2501 \"b\"\n        uid=1_2502 \" and \"\n        uid=1_2503 \"b\"\n        uid=1_2504 \" is less than \"\n        uid=1_2505 \"c\"\n        uid=1_2506 \".\"\n        uid=1_2507 link \"[119]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-AutoNT-65-127\"\n        uid=1_2511 \" C-derived languages interpret this expression differently: in C, the expression would first evaluate \"\n        uid=1_2512 \"a < b\"\n        uid=1_2513 \", resulting in 0 or 1, and that result would then be compared with \"\n        uid=1_2514 \"c\"\n        uid=1_2515 \".\"\n        uid=1_2516 link \"[120]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-CPL-128\"\n        uid=1_2520 \"Python uses \"\n        uid=1_2521 link \"arbitrary-precision arithmetic\" description=\"Arbitrary-precision arithmetic\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arbitrary-precision_arithmetic\"\n        uid=1_2523 \" for all integer operations. The \"\n        uid=1_2524 \"Decimal\"\n        uid=1_2525 \" type/class in the \"\n        uid=1_2526 \"decimal\"\n        uid=1_2527 \" module provides \"\n        uid=1_2528 link \"decimal floating-point numbers\" description=\"Decimal floating point\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Decimal_floating_point\"\n        uid=1_2530 \" to a pre-defined arbitrary precision with several rounding modes.\"\n        uid=1_2531 link \"[121]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-AutoNT-88-129\"\n        uid=1_2535 \" The \"\n        uid=1_2536 \"Fraction\"\n        uid=1_2537 \" class in the \"\n        uid=1_2538 \"fractions\"\n        uid=1_2539 \" module provides arbitrary precision for \"\n        uid=1_2540 link \"rational numbers\" description=\"Rational number\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rational_number\"\n        uid=1_2542 \".\"\n        uid=1_2543 link \"[122]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-130\"\n        uid=1_2547 \"Due to Python's extensive mathematics library and the third-party library \"\n        uid=1_2548 link \"NumPy\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/NumPy\"\n        uid=1_2550 \", the language is frequently used for scientific scripting in tasks such as numerical data processing and manipulation.\"\n        uid=1_2551 link \"[123]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-131\"\n        uid=1_2555 link \"[124]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-132\"\n      uid=1_2559 region \"Function syntax\"\n        uid=1_2560 heading \"Function syntax\" level=\"3\"\n        uid=1_2561 link \"edit\" description=\"Edit section: Function syntax\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Python_(programming_language)&action=edit&section=10\"\n        uid=1_2563 link \"Functions\" description=\"Function (computer programming)\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Function_(computer_programming)\"\n        uid=1_2565 \" are created in Python by using the \"\n        uid=1_2566 \"def\"\n        uid=1_2567 \" keyword. A function is defined similarly to how it is called, by first providing the function name and then the required parameters. Here is an example of a function that prints its inputs:\"\n        uid=1_2568 \"def\"\n        uid=1_2569 \" \"\n        uid=1_2570 \"printer\"\n        uid=1_2571 \"(\"\n        uid=1_2572 \"input1\"\n        uid=1_2573 \",\"\n        uid=1_2574 \" \"\n        uid=1_2575 \"input2\"\n        uid=1_2576 \" \"\n        uid=1_2577 \"=\"\n        uid=1_2578 \" \"\n        uid=1_2579 \"\"already there\"\"\n        uid=1_2580 \"):\"\n        uid=1_2581 \"\n    \"\n        uid=1_2582 \"print\"\n        uid=1_2583 \"(\"\n        uid=1_2584 \"input1\"\n        uid=1_2585 \")\"\n        uid=1_2586 \"\n    \"\n        uid=1_2587 \"print\"\n        uid=1_2588 \"(\"\n        uid=1_2589 \"input2\"\n        uid=1_2590 \")\"\n        uid=1_2591 \"\n\n\"\n        uid=1_2592 \"printer\"\n        uid=1_2593 \"(\"\n        uid=1_2594 \"\"hello\"\"\n        uid=1_2595 \")\"\n        uid=1_2596 \"\n\n\"\n        uid=1_2597 \"# Example output:\"\n        uid=1_2598 \"\n\"\n        uid=1_2599 \"# hello\"\n        uid=1_2600 \"\n\"\n        uid=1_2601 \"# already there\"\n        uid=1_2602 \"\n\"\n        uid=1_2603 \"To assign a default value to a function parameter in case no actual value is provided at run time, variable-definition syntax can be used inside the function header.\"\n    uid=1_2604 region \"Code examples\"\n      uid=1_2605 heading \"Code examples\" level=\"2\"\n      uid=1_2606 link \"edit\" description=\"Edit section: Code examples\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Python_(programming_language)&action=edit&section=11\"\n      uid=1_2608 link \"\"Hello, World!\" program\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/%22Hello,_World!%22_program\"\n      uid=1_2610 \":\"\n      uid=1_2611 \"print\"\n      uid=1_2612 \"(\"\n      uid=1_2613 \"'Hello, World!'\"\n      uid=1_2614 \")\"\n      uid=1_2615 \"\n\"\n      uid=1_2616 \"Program to calculate the \"\n      uid=1_2617 link \"factorial\" description=\"Factorial\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Factorial\"\n      uid=1_2619 \" of a non-negative integer:\"\n      uid=1_546 \"1\"\n      uid=1_2620 \"text\"\n      uid=1_2621 \" \"\n      uid=1_2622 \"=\"\n      uid=1_2623 \" \"\n      uid=1_2624 \"input\"\n      uid=1_2625 \"(\"\n      uid=1_2626 \"'Type a number, and its factorial will be printed: '\"\n      uid=1_2627 \")\"\n      uid=1_2628 \"\n\"\n      uid=1_546 \"2\"\n      uid=1_2629 \"\n\"\n      uid=1_546 \"3\"\n      uid=1_2630 \"if\"\n      uid=1_2631 \" \"\n      uid=1_2632 \"text\"\n      uid=1_2633 \".\"\n      uid=1_2634 \"isdigit\"\n      uid=1_2635 \"():\"\n      uid=1_2636 \"\n\"\n      uid=1_546 \"4\"\n      uid=1_2637 \"    \"\n      uid=1_2638 \"n\"\n      uid=1_2639 \" \"\n      uid=1_2640 \"=\"\n      uid=1_2641 \" \"\n      uid=1_2642 \"int\"\n      uid=1_2643 \"(\"\n      uid=1_2644 \"text\"\n      uid=1_2645 \")\"\n      uid=1_2646 \"\n\"\n      uid=1_546 \"5\"\n      uid=1_2647 \"else\"\n      uid=1_2648 \":\"\n      uid=1_2649 \"\n\"\n      uid=1_546 \"6\"\n      uid=1_2650 \"    \"\n      uid=1_2651 \"raise\"\n      uid=1_2652 \" \"\n      uid=1_2653 \"TypeError\"\n      uid=1_2654 \"(\"\n      uid=1_2655 \"'You must input a number'\"\n      uid=1_2656 \")\"\n      uid=1_2657 \"\n\"\n      uid=1_546 \"7\"\n      uid=1_2658 \"\n\"\n      uid=1_546 \"8\"\n      uid=1_2659 \"if\"\n      uid=1_2660 \" \"\n      uid=1_2661 \"n\"\n      uid=1_2662 \" \"\n      uid=1_2663 \"<\"\n      uid=1_2664 \" \"\n      uid=1_2665 \"0\"\n      uid=1_2666 \":\"\n      uid=1_2667 \"\n\"\n      uid=1_546 \"9\"\n      uid=1_2668 \"    \"\n      uid=1_2669 \"raise\"\n      uid=1_2670 \" \"\n      uid=1_2671 \"ValueError\"\n      uid=1_2672 \"(\"\n      uid=1_2673 \"'You must enter a non-negative integer'\"\n      uid=1_2674 \")\"\n      uid=1_2675 \"\n\"\n      uid=1_546 \"10\"\n      uid=1_2676 \"\n\"\n      uid=1_546 \"11\"\n      uid=1_2677 \"factorial\"\n      uid=1_2678 \" \"\n      uid=1_2679 \"=\"\n      uid=1_2680 \" \"\n      uid=1_2681 \"1\"\n      uid=1_2682 \"\n\"\n      uid=1_546 \"12\"\n      uid=1_2683 \"for\"\n      uid=1_2684 \" \"\n      uid=1_2685 \"i\"\n      uid=1_2686 \" \"\n      uid=1_2687 \"in\"\n      uid=1_2688 \" \"\n      uid=1_2689 \"range\"\n      uid=1_2690 \"(\"\n      uid=1_2691 \"2\"\n      uid=1_2692 \",\"\n      uid=1_2693 \" \"\n      uid=1_2694 \"n\"\n      uid=1_2695 \" \"\n      uid=1_2696 \"+\"\n      uid=1_2697 \" \"\n      uid=1_2698 \"1\"\n      uid=1_2699 \"):\"\n      uid=1_2700 \"\n\"\n      uid=1_546 \"13\"\n      uid=1_2701 \"    \"\n      uid=1_2702 \"factorial\"\n      uid=1_2703 \" \"\n      uid=1_2704 \"*=\"\n      uid=1_2705 \" \"\n      uid=1_2706 \"i\"\n      uid=1_2707 \"\n\"\n      uid=1_546 \"14\"\n      uid=1_2708 \"\n\"\n      uid=1_546 \"15\"\n      uid=1_2709 \"print\"\n      uid=1_2710 \"(\"\n      uid=1_2711 \"factorial\"\n      uid=1_2712 \")\"\n      uid=1_2713 \"\n\"\n    uid=1_2714 region \"Libraries\"\n      uid=1_2715 heading \"Libraries\" level=\"2\"\n      uid=1_2716 link \"edit\" description=\"Edit section: Libraries\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Python_(programming_language)&action=edit&section=12\"\n      uid=1_2718 \"Python's large standard library\"\n      uid=1_2719 link \"[125]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-AutoNT-86-133\"\n      uid=1_2723 \" is commonly cited as one of its greatest strengths. For Internet-facing applications, many standard formats and protocols such as \"\n      uid=1_2724 link \"MIME\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/MIME\"\n      uid=1_2726 \" and \"\n      uid=1_2727 link \"HTTP\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/HTTP\"\n      uid=1_2729 \" are supported. The language includes modules for creating \"\n      uid=1_2730 link \"graphical user interfaces\" description=\"Graphical user interface\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Graphical_user_interface\"\n      uid=1_2732 \", connecting to \"\n      uid=1_2733 link \"relational databases\" description=\"Relational database\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Relational_database\"\n      uid=1_2735 \", \"\n      uid=1_2736 link \"generating pseudorandom numbers\" description=\"Pseudorandom number generator\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pseudorandom_number_generator\"\n      uid=1_2738 \", arithmetic with arbitrary-precision decimals,\"\n      uid=1_2739 link \"[121]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-AutoNT-88-129\"\n      uid=1_2743 \" manipulating \"\n      uid=1_2744 link \"regular expressions\" description=\"Regular expression\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Regular_expression\"\n      uid=1_2746 \", and \"\n      uid=1_2747 link \"unit testing\" description=\"Unit testing\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Unit_testing\"\n      uid=1_2749 \".\"\n      uid=1_2750 \"Some parts of the standard library are covered by specifications—for example, the \"\n      uid=1_2751 link \"Web Server Gateway Interface\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Web_Server_Gateway_Interface\"\n      uid=1_2753 \" (WSGI) implementation \"\n      uid=1_2754 \"wsgiref\"\n      uid=1_2755 \" follows PEP 333\"\n      uid=1_2756 link \"[126]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-AutoNT-89-134\"\n      uid=1_2760 \"—but most parts are specified by their code, internal documentation, and \"\n      uid=1_2761 link \"test suites\" description=\"Test suite\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Test_suite\"\n      uid=1_2763 \". However, because most of the standard library is cross-platform Python code, only a few modules must be altered or rewritten for variant implementations.\"\n      uid=1_2764 \"As of 1\"\n      uid=1_2765 \" \"\n      uid=1_2766 \"October\"\n      uid=1_2767 \" \"\n      uid=1_2768 \"2026\"\n      uid=1_2769 \",\"\n      uid=1_2770 \" the \"\n      uid=1_2771 link \"Python Package Index\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_Package_Index\"\n      uid=1_2773 \" (PyPI), the official repository for third-party Python software, contains over 905,649\"\n      uid=1_2774 link \"[127]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-PyPI-135\"\n      uid=1_2778 \" projects.\"\n    uid=1_2779 region \"Development environments\"\n      uid=1_2780 heading \"Development environments\" level=\"2\"\n      uid=1_2781 link \"edit\" description=\"Edit section: Development environments\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Python_(programming_language)&action=edit&section=13\"\n      uid=1_2783 \"See also: \"\n      uid=1_2784 link \"Comparison of integrated development environments § Python\" description=\"Comparison of integrated development environments\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Comparison_of_integrated_development_environments#Python\"\n      uid=1_2788 \"Most\"\n      uid=1_2789 \"[\"\n      uid=1_2790 link \"which?\" description=\"Wikipedia:Avoid weasel words\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Avoid_weasel_words\"\n      uid=1_2792 \"]\"\n      uid=1_2793 \" Python implementations (including CPython) include a \"\n      uid=1_2794 link \"read–eval–print loop\" description=\"Read–eval–print loop\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Read%E2%80%93eval%E2%80%93print_loop\"\n      uid=1_2796 \" (REPL); this permits the environment to function as a \"\n      uid=1_2797 link \"command line interpreter\" description=\"Command line interpreter\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Command_line_interpreter\"\n      uid=1_2799 \", with which users enter statements sequentially and receive results immediately.\"\n      uid=1_2800 link \"[128]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-136\"\n      uid=1_2804 \"Also, CPython is bundled with an \"\n      uid=1_2805 link \"integrated development environment (IDE)\" description=\"Integrated development environment\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Integrated_development_environment\"\n      uid=1_2807 \" called \"\n      uid=1_2808 link \"IDLE\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/IDLE\"\n      uid=1_2810 \",\"\n      uid=1_2811 link \"[129]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-idle-137\"\n      uid=1_2815 \" which is oriented toward beginners.\"\n      uid=1_2816 \"[\"\n      uid=1_2817 link \"citation needed\" description=\"Wikipedia:Citation needed\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Citation_needed\"\n      uid=1_2819 \"]\"\n      uid=1_2820 \"[\"\n      uid=1_2821 link \"by whom?\" description=\"Wikipedia:Manual of Style/Words to watch\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Manual_of_Style/Words_to_watch#Unsupported_attributions\"\n      uid=1_2823 \"]\"\n      uid=1_2824 \"Other shells, including \"\n      uid=1_2825 link \"IDLE\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/IDLE\"\n      uid=1_2827 \" and \"\n      uid=1_2828 link \"IPython\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/IPython\"\n      uid=1_2830 \", add additional capabilities such as improved auto-completion, session-state retention, and \"\n      uid=1_2831 link \"syntax highlighting\" description=\"Syntax highlighting\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Syntax_highlighting\"\n      uid=1_2833 \".\"\n      uid=1_2834 link \"[129]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-idle-137\"\n      uid=1_2838 link \"[130]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-138\"\n      uid=1_2842 \"Standard desktop IDEs include \"\n      uid=1_2843 link \"PyCharm\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/PyCharm\"\n      uid=1_2845 \", \"\n      uid=1_2846 link \"Spyder\" description=\"Spyder (software)\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spyder_(software)\"\n      uid=1_2848 \", and \"\n      uid=1_2849 link \"Visual Studio Code\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Visual_Studio_Code\"\n      uid=1_2851 \";\"\n      uid=1_2852 link \"[131]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-139\"\n      uid=1_2856 \" there are \"\n      uid=1_2857 link \"web browser\" description=\"Web browser\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Web_browser\"\n      uid=1_2859 \"-based IDEs, such as the following environments:\"\n      uid=1_2860 link \"Jupyter Notebooks\" description=\"Project Jupyter\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Project_Jupyter\"\n      uid=1_2862 \", an open-source interactive computing platform;\"\n      uid=1_2863 link \"[132]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-140\"\n      uid=1_2867 link \"PythonAnywhere\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/PythonAnywhere\"\n      uid=1_2869 \", a browser-based IDE and hosting environment; and\"\n      uid=1_2870 \"Canopy, a commercial IDE from \"\n      uid=1_2871 link \"Enthought\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Enthought\"\n      uid=1_2873 \" that emphasizes \"\n      uid=1_2874 link \"scientific computing\" description=\"Scientific computing\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Scientific_computing\"\n      uid=1_2876 \".\"\n      uid=1_2877 link \"[133]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-141\"\n      uid=1_2881 link \"[134]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-142\"\n    uid=1_2885 region \"Implementations\"\n      uid=1_2886 heading \"Implementations\" level=\"2\"\n      uid=1_2887 link \"edit\" description=\"Edit section: Implementations\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Python_(programming_language)&action=edit&section=14\"\n      uid=1_2889 \"See also: \"\n      uid=1_2890 link \"List of Python software § Python implementations\" description=\"List of Python software\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_Python_software#Python_implementations\"\n      uid=1_2894 region \"Reference implementation\"\n        uid=1_2895 heading \"Reference implementation\" level=\"3\"\n        uid=1_2896 link \"edit\" description=\"Edit section: Reference implementation\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Python_(programming_language)&action=edit&section=15\"\n        uid=1_2898 link \"CPython\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/CPython\"\n        uid=1_2900 \" is the \"\n        uid=1_2901 link \"reference implementation\" description=\"Reference implementation\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Reference_implementation\"\n        uid=1_2903 \" of Python. This implementation is written in C, meaning the \"\n        uid=1_2904 link \"C11\" description=\"C11 (C standard revision)\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/C11_(C_standard_revision)\"\n        uid=1_2906 \" standard\"\n        uid=1_2907 link \"[135]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-143\"\n        uid=1_2911 \" since version 3.11. \"\n        uid=1_2912 \"Third-party extensions can be implemented using C99 or newer or C++.\"\n        uid=1_2913 link \"[136]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-144\"\n        uid=1_2917 link \"[137]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-AutoNT-66-145\"\n        uid=1_2921 \" CPython \"\n        uid=1_2922 link \"compiles\" description=\"Compiler\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Compiler\"\n        uid=1_2924 \" Python programs into an intermediate \"\n        uid=1_2925 link \"bytecode\" description=\"Bytecode\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bytecode\"\n        uid=1_2927 \",\"\n        uid=1_2928 link \"[138]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-AutoNT-67-146\"\n        uid=1_2932 \" which is then executed by a \"\n        uid=1_2933 link \"virtual machine\" description=\"Virtual machine\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Virtual_machine\"\n        uid=1_2935 \".\"\n        uid=1_2936 link \"[139]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-AutoNT-68-147\"\n        uid=1_2940 \" CPython is distributed with a large standard library written in a combination of C and native Python.\"\n        uid=1_2941 \"CPython is available for many platforms, including Windows and most modern \"\n        uid=1_2942 link \"Unix-like\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Unix-like\"\n        uid=1_2944 \" systems, including macOS (and \"\n        uid=1_2945 link \"Apple M1\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apple_M1\"\n        uid=1_2947 \" Macs\"\n        uid=1_2948 \"). \"\n        uid=1_2949 \"Starting with Python\"\n        uid=1_2950 \" \"\n        uid=1_2951 \"3.9, the Python installer intentionally fails to install on \"\n        uid=1_2952 link \"Windows 7\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Windows_7\"\n        uid=1_2954 \" and 8\"\n        uid=1_2955 \";\"\n        uid=1_2956 link \"[140]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-148\"\n        uid=1_2960 link \"[141]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-149\"\n        uid=1_2964 \" \"\n        uid=1_2965 link \"Windows XP\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Windows_XP\"\n        uid=1_2967 \" was supported until Python\"\n        uid=1_2968 \" \"\n        uid=1_2969 \"3.5.\"\n        uid=1_2970 \" Old discontinued Python versions unofficially support \"\n        uid=1_2971 link \"VMS\" description=\"OpenVMS\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/OpenVMS\"\n        uid=1_2973 \" (or mostly supporting\"\n        uid=1_2974 link \"[142]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-150\"\n        uid=1_2978 \") and \"\n        uid=1_2979 link \"OpenVMS\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/OpenVMS\"\n        uid=1_2981 \" x86-64 has support for now-discontinued Python 3.10.\"\n        uid=1_2982 link \"[143]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-151\"\n        uid=1_2986 link \"[144]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-152\"\n        uid=1_2990 \" Platform portability was one of Python's earliest priorities.\"\n        uid=1_2991 link \"[145]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-AutoNT-69-153\"\n        uid=1_2995 \" During development of Python\"\n        uid=1_2996 \" \"\n        uid=1_2997 \"1 and 2, even \"\n        uid=1_2998 link \"OS/2\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/OS/2\"\n        uid=1_3000 \" and \"\n        uid=1_3001 link \"Solaris\" description=\"Solaris (operating system)\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Solaris_(operating_system)\"\n        uid=1_3003 \" were supported;\"\n        uid=1_3004 link \"[8]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-DownloadOther-9\"\n        uid=1_3008 \" since that time, support has been dropped for many platforms.\"\n        uid=1_3009 \"All current Python versions (since 3.7) support only operating systems that feature \"\n        uid=1_3010 link \"multithreading\" description=\"Multithreading (software)\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multithreading_(software)\"\n        uid=1_3012 \" (and since 3.13, and improved in 3.14 optional \"free-threaded mode\"\"\n        uid=1_3013 link \"[146]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-154\"\n        uid=1_3017 \" used when opted into with \"\n        uid=1_3018 \"python3.14t\"\n        uid=1_3019 \") \"\n        uid=1_3020 \", by now supporting not nearly as many operating systems (dropping many outdated) than in the past.\"\n      uid=1_3021 region \"Other implementations\"\n        uid=1_3022 heading \"Other implementations\" level=\"3\"\n        uid=1_3023 link \"edit\" description=\"Edit section: Other implementations\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Python_(programming_language)&action=edit&section=16\"\n        uid=1_3025 \"All alternative implementations have at least slightly different semantics. For example, an alternative may include unordered dictionaries\"\n        uid=1_3026 \", in contrast to other current Python versions. As another example in the larger Python ecosystem, PyPy does not support the full C Python API.\"\n        uid=1_3027 \"Creating an executable with Python often is done by bundling an entire Python interpreter into the executable, which causes binary sizes to be massive for small programs,\"\n        uid=1_3028 link \"[147]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-155\"\n        uid=1_3032 \" yet there exist implementations that are capable of truly compiling Python. Alternative implementations include the following:\"\n        uid=1_3033 link \"PyPy\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/PyPy\"\n        uid=1_3035 \" is a faster, compliant interpreter of Python\"\n        uid=1_3036 \" \"\n        uid=1_3037 \"3.12, 3.11 and 2.7.\"\n        uid=1_3038 link \"[148]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-AutoNT-70-156\"\n        uid=1_3042 link \"[149]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-157\"\n        uid=1_3046 \" PyPy's \"\n        uid=1_3047 link \"just-in-time compiler\" description=\"Just-in-time compiler\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Just-in-time_compiler\"\n        uid=1_3049 \" often improves speed significantly relative to CPython, but PyPy does not support some libraries written in C.\"\n        uid=1_3050 link \"[150]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-AutoNT-71-158\"\n        uid=1_3054 \" PyPy offers support for the \"\n        uid=1_3055 link \"RISC-V\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/RISC-V\"\n        uid=1_3057 \" instruction-set architecture.\"\n        uid=1_3058 \"Codon\"\n        uid=1_3059 link \"[151]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-159\"\n        uid=1_3063 \" is an implementation with an \"\n        uid=1_3064 link \"ahead-of-time (AOT) compiler\" description=\"Ahead-of-time compilation\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ahead-of-time_compilation\"\n        uid=1_3066 \", which compiles a statically-typed Python-like language whose \"syntax and semantics are nearly identical to Python's, there are some notable differences\"\"\n        uid=1_3067 link \"[152]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-160\"\n        uid=1_3071 \" For example, Codon uses 64-bit machine integers for speed, not arbitrarily as with Python; Codon developers claim that speedups over CPython are usually on the order of ten to a hundred times. Codon compiles to machine code (via \"\n        uid=1_3072 link \"LLVM\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/LLVM\"\n        uid=1_3074 \") and supports native \"\n        uid=1_3075 link \"multithreading\" description=\"Multithreading (software)\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multithreading_(software)\"\n        uid=1_3077 \".\"\n        uid=1_3078 link \"[153]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-161\"\n        uid=1_3082 \"Codon can also compile to Python extension modules that can be imported and used from Python.\"\n        uid=1_3083 link \"MicroPython\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/MicroPython\"\n        uid=1_3085 \" and \"\n        uid=1_3086 link \"CircuitPython\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/CircuitPython\"\n        uid=1_3088 \" are Python\"\n        uid=1_3089 \" \"\n        uid=1_3090 \"3 variants that are optimized for \"\n        uid=1_3091 link \"microcontrollers\" description=\"Microcontroller\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Microcontroller\"\n        uid=1_3093 \", including the \"\n        uid=1_3094 link \"Lego Mindstorms EV3\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lego_Mindstorms_EV3\"\n        uid=1_3096 \".\"\n        uid=1_3097 link \"[154]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-162\"\n        uid=1_3101 \"Pyston is a variant of the Python runtime that uses just-in-time compilation to speed up execution of Python programs.\"\n        uid=1_3102 link \"[155]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-163\"\n        uid=1_3106 \"Cinder is a performance-oriented fork of CPython 3.8 that features a number of optimizations, including bytecode inline caching, eager evaluation of coroutines, a method-at-a-time \"\n        uid=1_3107 link \"JIT\" description=\"Just-in-time compilation\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Just-in-time_compilation\"\n        uid=1_3109 \", and an experimental bytecode compiler.\"\n        uid=1_3110 link \"[156]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-164\"\n        uid=1_3114 \"The Snek\"\n        uid=1_3115 link \"[157]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-165\"\n        uid=1_3119 link \"[158]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-166\"\n        uid=1_3123 link \"[159]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-167\"\n        uid=1_3127 \" embedded computing language \"is Python-inspired, but it is not Python. It is possible to write Snek programs that run under a full Python system, but most Python programs will not run under Snek.\"\"\n        uid=1_3128 link \"[160]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-168\"\n        uid=1_3132 \" Snek is compatible with 8-bit \"\n        uid=1_3133 link \"AVR microcontrollers\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AVR_microcontrollers\"\n        uid=1_3135 \" such as \"\n        uid=1_3136 link \"ATmega 328P\" description=\"ATmega\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ATmega\"\n        uid=1_3138 \"-based Arduino, as well as larger microcontrollers that are compatible with \"\n        uid=1_3139 link \"MicroPython\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/MicroPython\"\n        uid=1_3141 \". Snek is an imperative language that (unlike Python) omits \"\n        uid=1_3142 link \"object-oriented programming\" description=\"Object-oriented programming\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Object-oriented_programming\"\n        uid=1_3144 \". Snek supports only one numeric data type, which features 32-bit \"\n        uid=1_3145 link \"single precision\" description=\"Single precision\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Single_precision\"\n        uid=1_3147 \" (resembling \"\n        uid=1_3148 link \"JavaScript\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/JavaScript\"\n        uid=1_3150 \" numbers, though smaller).\"\n        uid=1_3151 \"RustPython is an implementation written in \"\n        uid=1_3152 link \"Rust\" description=\"Rust (programming language)\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rust_(programming_language)\"\n        uid=1_3154 \" language. It aims to be compatible with CPython, including its C-ABI.\"\n        uid=1_3155 link \"[161]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-169\"\n      uid=1_3159 region \"Unsupported implementations\"\n        uid=1_3160 heading \"Unsupported implementations\" level=\"3\"\n        uid=1_3161 link \"edit\" description=\"Edit section: Unsupported implementations\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Python_(programming_language)&action=edit&section=17\"\n        uid=1_3163 link \"Stackless Python\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stackless_Python\"\n        uid=1_3165 \" is a significant fork of CPython that implements \"\n        uid=1_3166 link \"microthreads\" description=\"Microthread\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Microthread\"\n        uid=1_3168 \". This implementation uses the \"\n        uid=1_3169 link \"call stack\" description=\"Call stack\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Call_stack\"\n        uid=1_3171 \" differently, thus allowing massively concurrent programs. PyPy also offers a stackless version.\"\n        uid=1_3172 link \"[162]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-AutoNT-73-170\"\n        uid=1_3176 \"Just-in-time Python compilers have been developed, but are now unsupported:\"\n        uid=1_3177 \"Google began a project named \"\n        uid=1_3178 link \"Unladen Swallow\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Unladen_Swallow\"\n        uid=1_3180 \" in 2009: this project aimed to speed up the Python interpreter five-fold by using \"\n        uid=1_3181 link \"LLVM\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/LLVM\"\n        uid=1_3183 \", and improve \"\n        uid=1_3184 link \"multithreading\" description=\"Multithreading (software)\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multithreading_(software)\"\n        uid=1_3186 \" capability for scaling to thousands of cores,\"\n        uid=1_3187 link \"[163]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-AutoNT-74-171\"\n        uid=1_3191 \" while typical implementations are limited by the \"\n        uid=1_3192 link \"global interpreter lock\" description=\"Global interpreter lock\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Global_interpreter_lock\"\n        uid=1_3194 \".\"\n        uid=1_3195 link \"Psyco\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Psyco\"\n        uid=1_3197 \" is a discontinued \"\n        uid=1_3198 link \"just-in-time\" description=\"Just-in-time compilation\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Just-in-time_compilation\"\n        uid=1_3200 \" \"\n        uid=1_3201 link \"specializing\" description=\"Run-time algorithm specialization\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Run-time_algorithm_specialization\"\n        uid=1_3203 \" compiler, which integrates with CPython and transforms bytecode to machine code at runtime. The emitted code is specialized for certain \"\n        uid=1_3204 link \"data types\" description=\"Data type\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Data_type\"\n        uid=1_3206 \" and is faster than standard Python code. Psyco does not support Python\"\n        uid=1_3207 \" \"\n        uid=1_3208 \"2.7 or later.\"\n        uid=1_3209 link \"PyS60\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/PyS60\"\n        uid=1_3211 \" was a Python\"\n        uid=1_3212 \" \"\n        uid=1_3213 \"2 interpreter for \"\n        uid=1_3214 link \"Series 60\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Series_60\"\n        uid=1_3216 \" mobile phones, which was released by \"\n        uid=1_3217 link \"Nokia\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nokia\"\n        uid=1_3219 \" in 2005. The interpreter implemented many modules from Python's standard library, as well as additional modules for integration with the \"\n        uid=1_3220 link \"Symbian\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symbian\"\n        uid=1_3222 \" operating system. The Nokia \"\n        uid=1_3223 link \"N900\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/N900\"\n        uid=1_3225 \" also supports Python through the \"\n        uid=1_3226 link \"GTK\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/GTK\"\n        uid=1_3228 \" widget library, allowing programs to be written and run on the target device.\"\n        uid=1_3229 link \"[164]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-172\"\n      uid=1_3233 region \"Transpilers to other languages\"\n        uid=1_3234 heading \"Transpilers to other languages\" level=\"3\"\n        uid=1_3235 link \"edit\" description=\"Edit section: Transpilers to other languages\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Python_(programming_language)&action=edit&section=18\"\n        uid=1_3237 \"There are several compilers/\"\n        uid=1_3238 link \"transpilers\" description=\"Transpiler\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Transpiler\"\n        uid=1_3240 \" to high-level object languages; the source language is unrestricted Python, a subset of Python, or a language similar to Python:\"\n        uid=1_3241 \"Brython\"\n        uid=1_3242 link \"[165]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-173\"\n        uid=1_3246 \" and Transcrypt\"\n        uid=1_3247 link \"[166]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-174\"\n        uid=1_3251 link \"[167]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-175\"\n        uid=1_3255 \" compile Python to \"\n        uid=1_3256 link \"JavaScript\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/JavaScript\"\n        uid=1_3258 \".\"\n        uid=1_3259 link \"Cython\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cython\"\n        uid=1_3261 \" compiles a superset of Python\"\n        uid=1_3262 \" to C. The resulting code can be used with Python via direct C-level API calls into the Python interpreter.\"\n        uid=1_3263 \"PyJL compiles/transpiles a subset of Python to \"human-readable, maintainable, and high-performance Julia source code\".\"\n        uid=1_3264 link \"[76]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-PyJL-79\"\n        uid=1_3268 \" Despite the developers' performance claims, this is not possible for \"\n        uid=1_3269 \"arbitrary\"\n        uid=1_3270 \" Python code; that is, compiling to a faster language or machine code is known to be impossible in the general case. The semantics of Python might potentially be changed, but in many cases speedup is possible with few or no changes in the Python code. The faster Julia source code can then be used from Python or compiled to machine code.\"\n        uid=1_3271 link \"Nuitka\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nuitka\"\n        uid=1_3273 \" compiles Python into C\"\n        uid=1_3274 \".\"\n        uid=1_3275 link \"[168]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-176\"\n        uid=1_3279 \" This compiler works with Python 3.4 to 3.14 (and 2.6 and 2.7) for Python's main supported platforms (and Windows 7 or even Windows XP) and for Android. The compiler developers claim full support for the now-discontinued Python 3.10, \"\n        uid=1_3280 \"partial support for Python 3.11 and 3.12, \"\n        uid=1_3281 \"and experimental support for Python 3.13. Nuitka supports macOS including Apple Silicon-based versions. \"\n        uid=1_3282 \"The compiler is free of cost, though it has commercial add-ons (e.g., for hiding source code).\"\n        uid=1_3283 link \"Numba\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Numba\"\n        uid=1_3285 \" is a JIT compiler that is used from Python; the compiler translates a subset of Python and NumPy code into fast machine code. This tool is enabled by adding a decorator to the relevant Python code.\"\n        uid=1_3286 \"Pythran compiles a subset of Python\"\n        uid=1_3287 \" \"\n        uid=1_3288 \"3 to C++ (\"\n        uid=1_3289 link \"C++11\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/C++11\"\n        uid=1_3291 \").\"\n        uid=1_3292 link \"[169]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-Guelton_Brunet_Amini_Merlini_2015_p=014001-177\"\n        uid=1_3296 link \"RPython\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/RPython\"\n        uid=1_3298 \" can be compiled to C, and it is used to build the PyPy interpreter for Python.\"\n        uid=1_3299 \"The Python → 11l → C++ transpiler\"\n        uid=1_3300 link \"[170]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-178\"\n        uid=1_3304 \" compiles a subset of Python\"\n        uid=1_3305 \" \"\n        uid=1_3306 \"3 to C++ (\"\n        uid=1_3307 link \"C++17\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/C++17\"\n        uid=1_3309 \").\"\n        uid=1_3310 \"There are also specialized compilers:\"\n        uid=1_3311 link \"MyHDL\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/MyHDL\"\n        uid=1_3313 \" is a Python-based \"\n        uid=1_3314 link \"hardware description language\" description=\"Hardware description language\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hardware_description_language\"\n        uid=1_3316 \" (HDL) that converts MyHDL code to \"\n        uid=1_3317 link \"Verilog\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Verilog\"\n        uid=1_3319 \" or \"\n        uid=1_3320 link \"VHDL\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/VHDL\"\n        uid=1_3322 \" code.\"\n        uid=1_3323 \"Some older projects existed, as well as compilers not designed for use with Python 3.x and related syntax:\"\n        uid=1_3324 \"Google's Grumpy \"\n        uid=1_3325 link \"transpiles\" description=\"Transpile\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Transpile\"\n        uid=1_3327 \" Python\"\n        uid=1_3328 \" \"\n        uid=1_3329 \"2 to \"\n        uid=1_3330 link \"Go\" description=\"Go (programming language)\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Go_(programming_language)\"\n        uid=1_3332 \".\"\n        uid=1_3333 link \"[171]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-179\"\n        uid=1_3337 link \"[172]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-180\"\n        uid=1_3341 link \"[173]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-181\"\n        uid=1_3345 \" The latest release was in 2017.\"\n        uid=1_3346 link \"IronPython\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/IronPython\"\n        uid=1_3348 \" allows running Python\"\n        uid=1_3349 \" \"\n        uid=1_3350 \"2.7 programs with the .NET \"\n        uid=1_3351 link \"Common Language Runtime\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Common_Language_Runtime\"\n        uid=1_3353 \".\"\n        uid=1_3354 link \"[174]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-182\"\n        uid=1_3358 \" An \"\n        uid=1_3359 link \"alpha\" description=\"Software release life cycle\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Software_release_life_cycle#Alpha\"\n        uid=1_3361 \" version (released in 2021), is available for \"Python\"\n        uid=1_3362 \" \"\n        uid=1_3363 \"3.4, although features and behaviors from later versions may be included.\"\"\n        uid=1_3364 link \"[175]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-183\"\n        uid=1_3368 link \"Jython\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jython\"\n        uid=1_3370 \" compiles Python\"\n        uid=1_3371 \" \"\n        uid=1_3372 \"2.7 to Java bytecode, allowing the use of Java libraries from a Python program.\"\n        uid=1_3373 link \"[176]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-184\"\n        uid=1_3377 link \"Pyrex\" description=\"Pyrex (programming language)\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pyrex_(programming_language)\"\n        uid=1_3379 \" (last released in 2010) and \"\n        uid=1_3380 link \"Shed Skin\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shed_Skin\"\n        uid=1_3382 \" (last released in 2013) compile to C and C++ respectively.\"\n      uid=1_3383 region \"Performance\"\n        uid=1_3384 heading \"Performance\" level=\"3\"\n        uid=1_3385 link \"edit\" description=\"Edit section: Performance\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Python_(programming_language)&action=edit&section=19\"\n        uid=1_3387 \"A performance comparison among various Python implementations, using a non-numerical (combinatorial) workload, was presented at EuroSciPy '13.\"\n        uid=1_3388 link \"[177]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-185\"\n        uid=1_3392 \" In addition, Python's performance relative to other programming languages is benchmarked by \"\n        uid=1_3393 link \"The Computer Language Benchmarks Game\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Computer_Language_Benchmarks_Game\"\n        uid=1_3395 \".\"\n        uid=1_3396 link \"[178]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-186\"\n        uid=1_3400 \"There are several approaches to optimizing Python performance, despite the inherent slowness of an \"\n        uid=1_3401 link \"interpreted language\" description=\"Interpreted language\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Interpreted_language\"\n        uid=1_3403 \". These approaches include the following strategies or tools:\"\n        uid=1_3404 link \"Just-in-time compilation\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Just-in-time_compilation\"\n        uid=1_3406 \": Dynamically compiling parts of a Python program during the execution of the program. This technique is used in libraries such as \"\n        uid=1_3407 link \"Numba\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Numba\"\n        uid=1_3409 \" and \"\n        uid=1_3410 link \"PyPy\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/PyPy\"\n        uid=1_3412 \".\"\n        uid=1_3413 link \"Static compilation\" description=\"Compiler\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Compiler\"\n        uid=1_3415 \": Sometimes, Python code can be compiled into machine code sometime before execution. An example of this approach is \"\n        uid=1_3416 link \"Cython\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cython\"\n        uid=1_3418 \", which compiles Python into C.\"\n        uid=1_3419 link \"Concurrency\" description=\"Concurrent computing\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Concurrent_computing\"\n        uid=1_3421 \" and \"\n        uid=1_3422 link \"parallelism\" description=\"Parallel computing\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Parallel_computing\"\n        uid=1_3424 \": Multiple tasks can be run simultaneously. Python contains modules such as `multiprocessing` to support this form of parallelism. Moreover, this approach helps to overcome limitations of the \"\n        uid=1_3425 link \"Global Interpreter Lock\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Global_Interpreter_Lock\"\n        uid=1_3427 \" (GIL) in CPU tasks.\"\n        uid=1_3428 \"Efficient \"\n        uid=1_3429 link \"data structures\" description=\"Data structures\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Data_structures\"\n        uid=1_3431 \": Performance can also be improved by using data types such as \"\n        uid=1_3432 \"Set\"\n        uid=1_3433 \" for membership tests, or \"\n        uid=1_3434 \"deque\"\n        uid=1_3435 \" from \"\n        uid=1_3436 \"collections\"\n        uid=1_3437 \" for \"\n        uid=1_3438 link \"queue\" description=\"Queueing theory\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Queueing_theory\"\n        uid=1_3440 \" operations.\"\n        uid=1_3441 \"Performance gains can be observed by utilizing libraries such as \"\n        uid=1_3442 link \"NumPy\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/NumPy\"\n        uid=1_3444 \". Most high-performance Python libraries use \"\n        uid=1_3445 link \"C\" description=\"C (programming language)\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/C_(programming_language)\"\n        uid=1_3447 \" or \"\n        uid=1_3448 link \"Fortran\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fortran\"\n        uid=1_3450 \" under the hood instead of the Python interpreter.\"\n        uid=1_3451 link \"[179]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-187\"\n    uid=1_3455 region \"Language development\"\n      uid=1_3456 heading \"Language development\" level=\"2\"\n      uid=1_3457 link \"edit\" description=\"Edit section: Language development\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Python_(programming_language)&action=edit&section=20\"\n      uid=1_3459 \"Python's development is conducted mostly through the \"\n      uid=1_3460 \"Python Enhancement Proposal\"\n      uid=1_3461 \" (PEP) process; this process is the primary mechanism for proposing major new features, collecting community input on issues, and documenting Python design decisions.\"\n      uid=1_3462 link \"[180]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-PepCite000-188\"\n      uid=1_3466 \" Python coding style is covered in PEP\"\n      uid=1_3467 \" \"\n      uid=1_3468 \"8.\"\n      uid=1_3469 link \"[87]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-pep8-94\"\n      uid=1_3473 \" Outstanding PEPs are reviewed and commented on by the Python community and the steering council.\"\n      uid=1_3474 link \"[180]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-PepCite000-188\"\n      uid=1_3478 \"Enhancement of the language corresponds with development of the CPython reference implementation. The mailing list python-dev is the primary forum for the language's development. Specific issues were originally discussed in the \"\n      uid=1_3479 link \"Roundup\" description=\"Roundup (issue tracker)\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roundup_(issue_tracker)\"\n      uid=1_3481 \" \"\n      uid=1_3482 link \"bug tracker\" description=\"Bug tracker\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bug_tracker\"\n      uid=1_3484 \" hosted by the foundation.\"\n      uid=1_3485 link \"[181]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-AutoNT-21-189\"\n      uid=1_3489 \" In 2022, all issues and discussions were migrated to \"\n      uid=1_3490 link \"GitHub\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/GitHub\"\n      uid=1_3492 \".\"\n      uid=1_3493 link \"[182]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-190\"\n      uid=1_3497 \" Development originally took place on a \"\n      uid=1_3498 link \"self-hosted\" description=\"Self-hosting (web services)\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Self-hosting_(web_services)\"\n      uid=1_3500 \" source-code repository running \"\n      uid=1_3501 link \"Mercurial\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mercurial\"\n      uid=1_3503 \", until Python moved to GitHub in January 2017.\"\n      uid=1_3504 link \"[183]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-py_dev_guide-191\"\n      uid=1_3508 \"CPython's public releases have three types, distinguished by which part of the version number is incremented:\"\n      uid=1_3509 \"Backward-incompatible versions\"\n      uid=1_3510 \", where code is expected to break and must be manually \"\n      uid=1_3511 link \"ported\" description=\"Ported\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ported\"\n      uid=1_3513 \". The first part of the version number is incremented. These releases happen infrequently—version 3.0 was released 8 years after 2.0. According to Guido van Rossum, a version 4.0 will probably never exist.\"\n      uid=1_3514 link \"[184]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-192\"\n      uid=1_3518 \"Major or \"feature\" releases\"\n      uid=1_3519 \" are largely compatible with the previous version but introduce new features. The second part of the version number is incremented. Starting with Python\"\n      uid=1_3520 \" \"\n      uid=1_3521 \"3.9, these releases are expected to occur annually.\"\n      uid=1_3522 link \"[185]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-193\"\n      uid=1_3526 link \"[186]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-194\"\n      uid=1_3530 \" Each major version is supported by bug fixes for several years after its release.\"\n      uid=1_3531 link \"[187]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-release-schedule-195\"\n      uid=1_3535 \"Bug fix releases\"\n      uid=1_3536 \",\"\n      uid=1_3537 link \"[188]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-AutoNT-22-196\"\n      uid=1_3541 \" which introduce no new features, occur approximately every three months; these releases are made when a sufficient number of bugs have been fixed \"\n      uid=1_3542 link \"upstream\" description=\"Upstream (software development)\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Upstream_(software_development)\"\n      uid=1_3544 \" since the last release. Security vulnerabilities are also patched in these releases. The third and final part of the version number is incremented.\"\n      uid=1_3545 link \"[188]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-AutoNT-22-196\"\n      uid=1_3549 \"Many \"\n      uid=1_3550 link \"alpha, beta, and release-candidates\" description=\"Beta release\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beta_release\"\n      uid=1_3552 \" are also released as previews and for testing before final releases. Although there is a rough schedule for releases, they are often delayed if the code is not ready yet. Python's development team monitors the state of the code by running a large \"\n      uid=1_3553 link \"unit test\" description=\"Unit test\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Unit_test\"\n      uid=1_3555 \" suite during development.\"\n      uid=1_3556 link \"[189]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-AutoNT-23-197\"\n      uid=1_3560 \"The major \"\n      uid=1_3561 link \"academic conference\" description=\"Academic conference\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Academic_conference\"\n      uid=1_3563 \" on Python is \"\n      uid=1_3564 link \"PyCon\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/PyCon\"\n      uid=1_3566 \". Also, there are special Python mentoring programs, such as \"\n      uid=1_3567 link \"PyLadies\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/PyLadies\"\n      uid=1_3569 \".\"\n    uid=1_3570 region \"Naming\"\n      uid=1_3571 heading \"Naming\" level=\"2\"\n      uid=1_3572 link \"edit\" description=\"Edit section: Naming\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Python_(programming_language)&action=edit&section=21\"\n      uid=1_3574 \"Python's name is inspired by the British comedy group \"\n      uid=1_3575 link \"Monty Python\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Monty_Python\"\n      uid=1_3577 \", whom Python creator Guido van Rossum enjoyed while developing the language. Monty Python references appear frequently in Python code and culture;\"\n      uid=1_3578 link \"[190]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-tutorial-chapter1-198\"\n      uid=1_3582 \" for example, the \"\n      uid=1_3583 link \"metasyntactic variables\" description=\"Metasyntactic variable\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Metasyntactic_variable\"\n      uid=1_3585 \" often used in Python literature are \"\n      uid=1_3586 link \"spam and eggs\" description=\"Spam (Monty Python sketch)\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spam_(Monty_Python_sketch)\"\n      uid=1_3590 \", rather than the traditional \"\n      uid=1_3591 link \"foo and bar\" description=\"Foobar\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foobar\"\n      uid=1_3595 \".\"\n      uid=1_3596 link \"[190]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-tutorial-chapter1-198\"\n      uid=1_3600 link \"[191]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-AutoNT-26-199\"\n      uid=1_3604 \" Also, the official Python documentation contains various references to Monty Python routines.\"\n      uid=1_3605 link \"[192]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-FOOTNOTELutz201317-200\"\n      uid=1_3609 link \"[193]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-201\"\n      uid=1_3613 \" Python users are sometimes referred to as \"Pythonistas\".\"\n      uid=1_3614 link \"[194]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-introducing_python-202\"\n    uid=1_3618 region \"Languages influenced by Python\"\n      uid=1_3619 heading \"Languages influenced by Python\" level=\"2\"\n      uid=1_3620 link \"edit\" description=\"Edit section: Languages influenced by Python\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Python_(programming_language)&action=edit&section=22\"\n      uid=1_3622 link \"Cobra\" description=\"Cobra (programming language)\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cobra_(programming_language)\"\n      uid=1_3624 \" has an \"\n      uid=1_3625 \"Acknowledgements\"\n      uid=1_3626 \" document that lists Python first among influencing languages.\"\n      uid=1_3627 link \"[195]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-203\"\n      uid=1_3631 link \"ECMAScript\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ECMAScript\"\n      uid=1_3633 \" and \"\n      uid=1_3634 link \"JavaScript\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/JavaScript\"\n      uid=1_3636 \" borrowed iterators and \"\n      uid=1_3637 link \"generators\" description=\"Generator (computer science)\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Generator_(computer_science)\"\n      uid=1_3639 \" from Python.\"\n      uid=1_3640 link \"[196]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-204\"\n      uid=1_3644 link \"Go\" description=\"Go (programming language)\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Go_(programming_language)\"\n      uid=1_3646 \" is designed for \"speed of working in a dynamic language like Python\".\"\n      uid=1_3647 link \"[197]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-205\"\n      uid=1_3651 link \"Julia\" description=\"Julia (programming language)\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Julia_(programming_language)\"\n      uid=1_3653 \" was designed to be \"as usable for general programming as Python\".\"\n      uid=1_3654 link \"[33]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-Julia-35\"\n      uid=1_3658 link \"Mojo\" description=\"Mojo (programming language)\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mojo_(programming_language)\"\n      uid=1_3660 \" is not a superset of Python,\"\n      uid=1_3661 link \"[198]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-206\"\n      uid=1_3665 \" though pre-1.0 versions were described as almost\"\n      uid=1_3666 link \"[34]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-Mojo-36\"\n      uid=1_3670 link \"[199]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-207\"\n      uid=1_3674 \" a superset of Python.\"\n      uid=1_3675 link \"[200]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-208\"\n      uid=1_3679 link \"GDScript\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/GDScript\"\n      uid=1_3681 \" is strongly influenced by Python.\"\n      uid=1_3682 link \"[201]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-209\"\n      uid=1_3686 link \"Groovy\" description=\"Apache Groovy\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apache_Groovy\"\n      uid=1_3688 \", \"\n      uid=1_3689 link \"Boo\" description=\"Boo (programming language)\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Boo_(programming_language)\"\n      uid=1_3691 \", \"\n      uid=1_3692 link \"CoffeeScript\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/CoffeeScript\"\n      uid=1_3694 \", \"\n      uid=1_3695 link \"F#\" description=\"F Sharp (programming language)\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/F_Sharp_(programming_language)\"\n      uid=1_3697 \", \"\n      uid=1_3698 link \"Nim\" description=\"Nim (programming language)\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nim_(programming_language)\"\n      uid=1_3700 \", \"\n      uid=1_3701 link \"Ruby\" description=\"Ruby (programming language)\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ruby_(programming_language)\"\n      uid=1_3703 \",\"\n      uid=1_3704 link \"[35]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-bini-37\"\n      uid=1_3708 \" \"\n      uid=1_3709 link \"Swift\" description=\"Swift (programming language)\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Swift_(programming_language)\"\n      uid=1_3711 \",\"\n      uid=1_3712 link \"[36]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-lattner2014-38\"\n      uid=1_3716 \" and \"\n      uid=1_3717 link \"V\" description=\"V (programming language)\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/V_(programming_language)\"\n      uid=1_3719 link \"[37]\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)#cite_note-vpeople-39\"\n      uid=1_3723 \" have been influenced, as well.\"\n    uid=1_3724 region \"See also\"\n      uid=1_3725 heading \"See also\" level=\"2\"\n      uid=1_3726 link \"edit\" description=\"Edit section: See also\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Python_(programming_language)&action=edit&section=23\"\n      uid=1_3728 navigation \"Portals\"\n        uid=1_3729 link \"icon\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Octicons-terminal.svg\"\n          uid=1_3730 image \"icon\" url=\"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6f/Octicons-terminal.svg/40px-Octicons-terminal.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail\"\n        uid=1_3731 link \"Computer programming portal\" description=\"Portal:Computer programming\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Portal:Computer_programming\"\n        uid=1_3733 link \"Free and open-source software portal\" description=\"Portal:Free and open-source software\" url=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Portal:Free_and_open-source_software\"\n      uid=1_3735 link \"List of machine learning and deep learning software for Python\" descripti